\h 1 Chronicles
\toc1 1 Chronicles
\toc2 1 Chronicles
\toc3 1 Chr.
\mt2 The first
\mt1 Sequential Account
\mt2 (1 Chronicles)
\is1 Introduction
\ip The \bk First\bk* and the \bk Second Account of Chronicles\bk* are a retelling of lots of what’s included in \bk The accounts about Shmuel (Samuel)\bk* and of \bk The accounts about the kings\bk*, but told in different words. There seem to be two primary purposes for this account:
\ip 1. So that the reader can learn more about the destruction of Yehudah (Judah) and Yisrael (Israel), as God fulfilled his promise and plans for his people. This writer illustrates the achievements of David, Shelomoh (Solomon), of Yehoshafat (Jehoshaphat), Hizkiyah (Hezekiah), of Yoshiyah (Josiah), along with other people who obeyed Yahweh’s instructions.
\ip 2. To document the division of the priests and Levites in preparation for their service in the soon-to-be-built temple for Yahweh, and the extensive preparations by King David that were eventually handed over to King Shelomoh. Yahweh had carefully instructed David about how he wanted the temple worship to operate, and then this was all written down and handed over to Shelomoh, who would be the one who would go on to construct the buildings and all the furnishings and equipment and utensils that were required for its functioning.
\iot Main components of this document
\io1 Lists of the ancestors \ior 1:1–9:44\ior*
\io1 King Sha’ul’s death \ior 10:1-14\ior*
\io1 David’s kingdom \ior 11:1–29:30\ior*
\io2 a. Wars and victories \ior 11:1–22:1\ior*
\io2 b. Preparation for the temple construction \ior 22:2–29:30\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\rem NOTE: This USFM file was exported from the ESFM source file with some loss of features, so don’t edit this derived file.
\c 1
\s1 Adam’s descendants
\r (Gen. 5:1-32; 10:1-32; 11:10-26)
\p
\v 1 Adam, Shet \add (Seth)\add*, Enosh,
\v 2 Keynan, Mahalalel, Yered \add (Jared)\add*,
\v 3 Hanok \add (Enoch)\add*, Metushelah \add (Methuselah)\add*, Lemek \add (Lamech)\add*,
\v 4 Noah, Shem, Ham, and Yefet \add (Japheth)\add*.
\p
\v 5 Yefet’s sons were Gomer, Magog, Maday, Yavan, Tuval, Meshek, and Tiras.
\v 6 Gomer’s sons were Ashkenaz, Rifat, and Togarmah.
\v 7 Yavan’s sons were Elishah, Tarshish, Kitim, and Dodanim.
\p
\v 8 Ham’s sons were Kush, Mitsrayim, Put, and Kena’an \add (Canaan)\add*.
\v 9 Kush’s sons were Seva, Havilah, Savta, Raemah, and Savteka. Raemah’s sons were Sheva and Dedan.
\v 10 Then Kush fathered Nimrod \add and\add* he became a mighty \add warrior\add*.
\v 11 Mitsrayim was the ancestor of the Lud, the Anamim, the Lehavim, the Naftuhim,
\v 12 the Patrusim, the Kasluhim, and the Kaftor people groups. (The Pelishtim descended from the Kasluhim people group.)
\v 13 Kena’an’s first son was Tsidon \add (Sidon)\add*, and then \add he was also the ancestor of the Hitim\add*,
\v 14 the Yevusim, the Amorim, the Girgashim,
\v 15 the Hivim, the Arkim, the Sinim,
\v 16 the Arvadim, the Tsemarim, and the Hamatim.
\p
\v 17 Shem’s sons were Eylam, Ashur, Arpakshad, Lud, and Aram. \add Aram’s sons were\add*: Uts \add (Uz)\add*, Hul, Geter, and Meshek.
\v 18 Arpakshad was the father of Shelah, and Shelah was the father of Ever.
\v 19 Ever had two sons: Peleg (\add which sounds like the Hebrew word for ‘divided’\add*, because in his lifetime, the earth was divided)\x + \xo 1:19: \xt Gen 11:1-9.\x* and Yaketan.
\v 20 Yaketan was the ancestor of: Almodad, Shelef, Hatsarmavet, Yerah,
\v 21 Hadoram, Uzal, Diklah,
\v 22 Eyval, Avimael, Sheva,
\v 23 Ofir, Havilah, and Yovav. All those were the descendants of Yaketan.
\p
\v 24 \add These were the descendants of\add* Shem \add (in order from him to Avraham\add* \add )\add*: Arpakshad, Shelah,
\v 25 Ever, Peleg, Re’u,
\v 26 Serug, Nahor, Terah,
\v 27 and Avram (\add whose name God later changed to\add* Avraham).
\s1 Yishmael’s descendants
\r (Gen. 25:12-16)
\p
\v 28 Avraham’s sons were Yitshak \add (Isaac)\add* and Yishmael \add (Ishmael)\add*.
\v 29 These were their family lines: The first son born to Yishmael \add (the son of Avraham’s slave-wife Hagar)\add* was Nevayot, then his other \add sons\add* Kedar, Adbe’el, Mivsam,
\v 30 Mishma, Dumah, Masa, Hadad, Teyma,
\v 31 Yetur, Nafish, and Kedemah. Those were Yishmael’s \add twelve\add* sons.
\v 32 The sons of Avraham’s slave-wife Keturah were Zimran, Yakeshan, Medan, Midyan, Yishbak, and Shuah. Yakeshan’s sons were Sheva and Dedan.
\v 33 Midyan’s sons were Eyfah, Efer, Hanok, Avida, and Elda’ah. All those were Keturah’s descendants.
\p
\v 34 Avraham had his son Yitshak \add (Isaac)\add* \add by his wife Sarah\add*, and Yitshak’s sons were Esav \add (Esau)\add* and \add Ya’akov\add* \add (Jacob)\add* \add , whose name God later changed to\add* Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*.
\s1 Esav’s descendants
\r (Gen. 36:1-19)
\p
\v 35 Esav’s \add (Esau)\add* sons were Elifaz, Re’uel, Ye’ush, Yaelam, and Korah.
\v 36 Elifaz’s sons were Teyman, Omar, Tsefo, Gaetam, Kenaz, Timna, and Amalek.
\v 37 Re’uel’s sons were Nahat, Zerah, Shamah, and Mizah.
\s1 Se’ir’s descendants
\r (Gen. 36:20-30)
\p
\v 38 Se’ir \add was another descendant of Esav\add* \add (Esau)\add* \add , and his\add* sons were Lotan, Shoval, Tsivoen, Anah, Dishon, Etser, and Dishan.
\v 39 Lotan’s sons were Hori and Homam, and Lotan’s sister was Timna.
\v 40 Shoval’s sons were Alvan, Manahat, Eyval, Shefo, and Onam. Tsivoen’s sons were Ayah and Anah.
\v 41 Anah’s son was Dishon. Dishon’s sons were Hamran, Eshban, Yitran, and Keran.
\v 42 Etser’s sons were Bilhan, Za’avan, and Ya’akan. Dishan’s sons were Uts \add (Uz)\add* and Aran.
\s1 Edom’s rulers
\r (Gen. 36:31-43)
\p
\v 43 These are the kings who ruled the region of Edom \add (where Se’ir’s descendants lived)\add* before any kings ruled over Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*: Bela son of Be’or, and his city’s name was Dinhavah.
\v 44 When Bela died, Zerah’s son Yovav from Batserah \add city\add* \add replaced him as king\add*.
\v 45 When Yovav died, Husham \add replaced him as king\add*. He was from the region where the Teyman people group lived.
\v 46 When Husham died, Bedad’s son Hadad \add replaced him as king\add*. His city’s name was Avit. \add Hadad\add* defeated the Midyan people group in the Moav \add (Moab)\add* region.
\v 47 When Hadad died, Samlah from Masrekah \add city\add* \add replaced him as king\add*.
\v 48 When Samlah died, Shaul from Rehovot-on-the-river \add replaced him as king\add*.
\v 49 When Shaul died, Akbor’s son Ba’al-Hanan \add replaced him as king\add*.
\v 50 When Ba’al-Hanan died, Hadad \add replaced him as king\add*. His city’s name was Paeu. His wife’s name was Meheytavel—she was the daughter of Matred \add and the granddaughter\add* of Mey-Zahav.
\p
\v 51 Then Hadad\x + \xo 1:51: \xt 1:50.\x* died.
\p The leaders of the Edom people group were Chiefs Timna, Alvah, Yetet,
\v 52 Oholivamah, Elah, Pinon,
\v 53 Kenaz, Teyman, Mivtsar,
\v 54 Magdiyel, and Iram. Those were the chiefs of Edom.
\c 2
\s1 Ya’akov’s descendants
\p
\v 1 These were Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* sons: Re’uven, Shim’on \add (Simeon)\add*, Levi, and Yehudah \add (Judah)\add*, Yisakar \add (Issachar)\add*, and Zevulun,
\v 2 Dan, Yosef \add (Joseph)\add*, Benyamin, Naftali \add (Naphtali)\add*, Gad, and Asher.
\s1 Yehudah’s descendants
\p
\v 3 Yehudah’s \add (Judah)\add* sons (with the Kena’ani \add (Canaanite)\add* \add woman\add* Bat-Shua) were Er, Onan, and Shela. (Yehudah’s eldest son, Er, was evil in Yahweh’s eyes, and he killed him.)
\v 4 His daughter-in-law Tamar had \add twin boys named\add* Perets and Zerah, so altogether Yehudah had five sons.
\p
\v 5 Perets’s sons were Hetsron and Hamul.
\v 6 Zerah had five sons: Zimri, Eytan, Heyman, Kalkol, and Dara.
\v 7 \add Zimri had a son named Karmi.\add* Karmi had a \add son\add* named Akar who brought trouble into Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* \add by violating a ban\add* on devoted things.\x + \xo 2:7: \xt Josh 7:1.\x*
\v 8 Eytan’s \add son\add* was Azaryah.
\s1 King David’s ancestors
\p
\v 9 Hetsron had three sons: Yerahme’el, Ram, and Keluvay.\f + \fr 2:9 \ft In the Hebrew, ‘Keluvay’ is called ‘Kalev’ (Caleb) below.\f*
\p
\v 10 Ram was the father of Aminadav, and Aminadav was the father of Nahshon—a leader of the \add tribe\add* of Yehudah \add (Judah)\add*.
\v 11 Nahshon was the father of Salma. Salma was the father of Boaz.
\v 12 Boaz was the father of Oved. Oved was the father of Yishay \add (Jesse)\add*.
\v 13 Yishay had his son Eliav first, Avinadav second, Shimea third.
\v 14 Netanel fourth, Raday fifth,
\v 15 Otsem sixth, then David \add last\add*.
\v 16 Their sisters were Tseruyah and Avigayil. Tseruyah’s three sons were Avishay, Yoav \add (Joab)\add*, and Asahel.
\v 17 Avigayil had Amasa. Amasa’s father was Yeter, a descendant of Yishmael \add (Ishmael)\add*.
\s1 The descendants of Kalev (Caleb)
\p
\v 18 Hetsron’s \add third\add* son Kalev \add (Caleb)\add* had sons with Azuvah, one of \add his\add* wives. The sons were Yesher, Shovav, and Ardon. (The other wife’s name was Yerieot.)
\v 19 When Azuvah died, Kalev married Efrat, and they had a son named Hur.
\v 20 Hur was the father of Uri, and Uri was the father of Betsalel.
\p
\v 21 Later, when Hetsron was sixty years old, he \add had sexual intercourse\add* with a daughter of Makir from the region of Gilead and married her. \add Hetsron’s new wife\add* gave birth to \add their son\add* Seguv.
\v 22 Seguv was the father of Yair, and \add Yair\add* controlled twenty-three cities in the Gilead region.
\v 23 \add But\add* Geshur and Aram captured Havot-Ya’ir and Kenat from them, plus \add their surrounding towns\add*—sixty towns \add in all,\add* \add populated by\add* descendants of Makir (the father of Gilead).
\v 24 After Hetsron’s death in Kalev-Efratah, \add his\add* wife Aviyah gave birth to his son Ashur \add (Assyria)\add*, the father of Tekoa.
\s1 Yerahme’el’s descendants
\p
\v 25 Hetsron’s oldest son was Yerahme’el \add and\add* his sons were Ram (the oldest), Bunah, Oren, Otsem, and Ahiyah.
\v 26 Yerahme’el had another wife named Atarah. She was the mother of Onam.
\v 27 The sons of Yerahme’el’s oldest son, Ram, were Ma’ats, Yamin, and Eker.
\v 28 Onam’s sons were Shamay and Yada, and Shamay’s sons were Nadav and Avishur.
\p
\v 29 Avishur’s wife was Avihayil, and \add their sons were\add* Ahban and Molid.
\v 30 Nadav’s sons were Seled and Apayim. (Seled died childless.)
\v 31 Apayim’s son was Yishei, Yishei’s son was Sheshan, and Sheshan’s son was Ahlay.
\p
\v 32 The sons of Shamay’s brother Yada were Yeter and Yonatan \add (Jonathan)\add*. (Yeter died childless.)
\v 33 Yonatan’s sons were Pelet and Zaza.
\m Those were the descendants of Yerahme’el.
\p
\v 34 Sheshan had no sons, only daughters. \add He\add* had a \add male\add* servant from Mitsrayim \add (Egypt)\add* whose name was Yarha,
\v 35 and Sheshan gave his daughter to \add him\add* for a wife, and \add they had a son\add* Atay.
\v 36 Atay was the father of Natan \add (Nathan)\add*, and Natan was the father of Zavad.
\v 37 Zavad was the father of Eflal, and Eflal was the father of Oved.
\v 38 Oved was the father of Yehu \add (Jehu)\add*, and Yehu was the father of Azaryah.
\v 39 Azaryah was the father of Helets, and Helets was the father of Eleasah.
\v 40 Eleasah was the father of Sismay, and Sismay was the father of Shalum.
\v 41 Shalum was the father of Yekamyah, and Yekamyah was the father of Elishama.
\s1 The other descendants of Kalev
\p
\v 42 The oldest son of Yerahme’el’s brother Kalev \add (Caleb)\add* was Meysha. \add Meysha\add* was the father of Zif, and Zif was the father of Mareshah. Mareshah was the father of Hevron.
\v 43 Hevron’s sons were Korah, Tapuah, Rekem, and Shema.
\v 44 Shema was the father of Raham. Raham was the father of Yarekeam. Rekem was the father of Shamay.
\v 45 Shamay’s son was Maeon, and Maeon was the father of Beyt-Tsur.
\v 46 Kalev had a slave-wife named Eyfah who gave birth to Haran, Motsa, and Gazez. Haran had a son \add who he also named\add* Gazez.
\v 47 Yehday’s sons were: Regem, Yotam \add (Jotham)\add*, Geyshan, Pelet, Eyfah, and Sha’af.
\p
\v 48 Kalev had \add another\add* slave-wife named Ma’akah who gave birth to Shever and Tirhanah.
\v 49 She also gave birth to Sha’af (the father of Madmanah), Sheva (father of Makbena and Givea), and Kalev’s daughter Aksah.
\p
\v 50 These people were \add also\add* descendants of Kalev: \add His other wife\add* Efratah had a son Hur. Hur’s sons were Shoval (\add founder\add* of Kiryat-Yearim \add city\add*),
\v 51 Salma (\add founder\add* of Beyt-Lehem \add (Bethlehem)\add*), \add and\add* Haref (\add founder\add* of Beyt-Gader).
\v 52 The descendants of Shoval (\add founder\add* of Kiryat-Yearim) were Haroeh, half of the Manahatim,
\v 53 and the clans from Kiryat-Yearim: the Yitrim, the Putim, the Shumatim, and the Mishraim. From those came the Tsor’atim and Eshtaolim.
\v 54 Salma’s descendants were \add the people of\add* Beyt-Lehem, the Netofatim, the clan of Atrot-Beyt-Yo’av, and half of the Manahatim, \add who were also\add* Tsor’im.
\v 55 The clans of the scribes lived at Yaebets: the Tiratim, the Shimeatim, and the Sukatim. These are the Keynim who descended from Hamat, the ancestor of Rekav’s family.
\c 3
\s1 King David’s children
\p
\v 1 These were David’s sons who were born to him in Hevron: the first was Amnon by Ahinoam the Yizre’eli, the second Daniyel by Avigayil the Karmeli,
\v 2 the third Avshalom \add (Absalom)\add*, the son of Ma’akah (daughter of King Talmay of Geshur), the fourth Adoniyah, the son of Hagit,
\v 3 the fifth Shefatyah by Avital, the sixth Yitream by his wife Eglah.
\m
\v 4 Six were born to \add David\add* in Hevron \add where\add* he reigned for seven and a half years.\x + \xo 3:4: \xt 2Sam 5:4-5; 1Ki 2:11; 1Ch 29:27.\x*
\p Then he reigned in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* for thirty-three years
\v 5 and these were born to him \add there\add*: Bat-Shua (\add also known as Bat-Sheva\add* \add (Bathsheba)\add*, daughter of Amiyel)\x + \xo 3:5: \xt 2Sam 11:3.\x* gave birth to four \add sons\add*: Shimea, Shovav, Natan \add (Nathan)\add*, and Shelomoh \add (Solomon)\add*.
\v 6 \add Other sons were\add* Yavehar, Elishama, Elifelet,
\v 7 Nogah, Nefeg, Yafia,
\v 8 Elishama, Elyada, and Elifelet. (Nine in all.)
\v 9 David also had sons from his slave-wives. Their sister was Tamar.
\s1 Descendants of King Shelomoh (Solomon)
\p
\v 10 Shelomoh \add (Solomon)\add* had a son Rehav’am \add (Rehoboam)\add*, he had a son Aviyah, he had a son Asa, he had a son Yehoshafat \add (Jehoshaphat)\add*,
\v 11 he had a son Yoram \add (Joram)\add*, he had a son Ahazyah, he had a son Yoash,
\v 12 he had a son Amatsyah, he had a son Azaryah, he had a son Yotam \add (Jotham)\add*,
\v 13 he had a son Ahaz, he had a son Hizkiyah \add (Hezekiah)\add*, he had a son Menasheh \add (Manasseh)\add*,
\v 14 he had a son Amon, \add and\add* he had a son Yoshiyah \add (Josiah)\add*.
\v 15 Yoshiyah had four sons: The oldest was Yohanan, second was Yehoyakim, third was Tsidkiyah \add (Zedekiah)\add*, \add and\add* the fourth was Shalum.
\v 16 Yehoyakim had a son Yekaneyah, \add and\add* he had a son Tsidkiyah.
\s1 Descendants of King Yekaneyah
\p
\v 17 Yekaneyah \add was captured and taken to Bavel\add* \add (Babylon)\add*. His sons were Shealtiyel,
\v 18 Malkiram, Pedayah, Shenatsar, Yekamyah, Hoshama, and Nedavyah.
\v 19 Pedayah’s sons were Zerubavel and Shimei. \add Two of\add* Zerubavel’s sons were Meshulam and Hananyah, and their sister was Shelomot.
\v 20 Zerubavel’s five \add other\add* \add sons\add* were Hashuvah, Ohel, Berekyah, Hasadyah, and Yushav-Hesed.
\v 21 Hananyah’s descendants were Pelatyah, Yeshayah \add (Isaiah)\add*, \add and\add* the sons of Refayah, of Arnan, of Ovadyah \add (Obadiah)\add*, and of Shekanyah.
\v 22 Shekanyah’s six descendants were Shemayah and Shemayah’s sons Hatush, Yigal, Bariah, Nearyah, and Shafat.
\v 23 Nearyah’s three sons were Elyehoeynay, Hizkiyah, and Azrikam.
\v 24 Elyehoeynay’s seven sons were Hodavyah, Elyashiv, Pelayah, Akuv, Yohanan, Delayah, and Anani.
\c 4
\s1 The other descendants of Yehudah (Judah)
\p
\v 1 Yehudah’s \add (Judah)\add* sons were Perets, Hetsron, Karmi, Hur, and Shoval.
\v 2 Shoval had a son Reayah, Reayah had a son Yahat, and Yahat had Ahumay and Lahad. Those were the ancestors of the Tsor’atim.
\v 3 Yizre’el \add (Jezreel)\add*, Yishma, and Yidbas \add started the clans that lived in\add* Eytam \add city\add*, and they had a sister named Hatslelponi.
\v 4 Hur was the first son born to \add his mother\add* Efratah, \add and\add* he \add founded\add* Beyt-Lehem \add (Bethlehem)\add* \add city\add*. Hur had these descendants: Penuel and Ezer. Penuel \add started the clans that lived in\add* Gedor \add city\add*, and Ezer \add started the clans that lived in\add* Hushah \add city\add*.
\v 5 \add Hetsron’s son\add* Ashur \add (Assyria)\add*, the father of Tekoa, had two wives, Helah and Na’arah,
\v 6 and Na’arah bore him Ahuzam, Hefer, Teymeni, and Ha’ahashtari.
\v 7 \add Ashur and his wife\add* Helah’s sons were Tseret, Yitshar, Etnan,
\v 8 and Kots. Kots was the father of Anuv and Tsovevah, and the ancestor of the clans descended from Harum’s son Aharhel.
\p
\v 9 Yaebets was more respected than his brothers. His mother had named him Yaebets \add (which sounds like the word meaning ‘pain’)\add*, saying, “It’s because I bore him in pain.”
\v 10 Yaebets called out to Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* god, saying, “Oh, please give me a real blessing, and \add help me\add* and enlarge my territory, and don’t \add do anything to harm me\add*, so that I won’t be in pain.” And God \add did what Yaebets requested\add*.
\p
\v 11 \add Another of Yehudah’s\add* \add (Judah)\add* \add descendants was\add* Shuhah. His brother Keluv was the father of Mehir. \add Mehir\add* was the father of Eshton.
\v 12 Eshton was the father of Beyt-Rafa, Paseah, and Tehinah. Tehinah \add founded\add* Nahash city, but \add lived in\add* Rekah.
\p
\v 13 Kenaz’s sons were Otniel and Serayah. And Otniel’s sons were Hatat \add and Me’onotay\add*.\f + \fr 4:13 \ft Not in the Hebrew, but ‘sons’ was plural, so it seems that it may have been accidentally dropped out of the text.\f*
\v 14 Me’onotay had a son Ofrah, and Serayah fathered Yoav \add (Joab)\add*, the \add founder\add* of Craftsmen’s Valley, because they were craftsmen.
\p
\v 15 The sons of Kalev \add (Caleb)\add* (Yefuneh’s son) were Iru, Elah, and Na’am, and Elah’s son was Kenaz.
\p
\v 16 Yehalel’el’s sons were Zif, Zifah, Tirya, and Asarel.
\p
\v 17 Ezra’s sons were Yeter, Mered, Efer, and Yalon. And \add Mered’s wife Bityah\add* conceived Miryam \add (Miriam)\add*, Shamay, and Yishbah, the father of Eshtemoa.
\v 18 \add She\add* was one of Par’oh’s \add (Pharaoh)\add* \add daughters\add*. Mered’s wife, who was a descendant of Yehudah \add (Judah)\add*, gave birth to Yered \add (Jared)\add* the father of Gedor, Hever the father of Sokoh, and Yekutiel the father of Zanoah.
\v 19 The sons of Hodiyah’s wife (Naham’s sister) were the father of Ke’ilah the Garmi and \add the father of\add* Eshtemoa the Ma’akati.
\p
\v 20 Shim’on’s sons were Amnon, Rinah, Ben-Hanan, and Tulon.
\p Yishei’s sons were Zohet and Ben-Zohet.
\s1 Shelah’s descendants
\p
\v 21 The sons of Shela (one of Yehudah’s \add (Judah)\add* sons) were Er (father of Lekah), Laedah (father of Mareshah and the clans \add who worked with\add* linen at Beyt-Eshbea),
\v 22 Yokim and the men of Kozava, and Yoash and Saraf, who ruled in Moav \add (Moab)\add* and Yashuvi-Lehem. (\add All this is from ancient records\add*.)
\v 23 They were the potters \add who lived in\add* Netaeim and Gederah—they lived there and \add worked for\add* the king.
\s1 The descendants of Shim’on (Simeon)
\p
\v 24 Shim’on’s \add (Simeon)\add* sons were Nemuel, Yamin, Yariv, Zerah, and Shaul.
\v 25 \add Shaul’s\add* son was Shalum. \add Shalum’s\add* son was Mivsam. \add Mivsam’s\add* son was Mishma.
\v 26 The \add following were\add* Mishma’s descendants: \add Mishma’s\add* son was Hamuel. \add Hamuel’s\add* son was Zakur. \add Zakur’s\add* son was Shimei.
\v 27 Shimei had sixteen sons and six daughters, but none of his brothers had many children, so \add Shim’on’s\add* \add descendants were never as numerous as those\add* of \add his younger brother\add* Yehudah \add (Judah)\add*.
\p
\v 28 \add Shim’on’s descendants\add* lived in \add these cities and towns\add*: Be’er-Sheva \add (Beersheba)\add*, Moladah, Hatsar-Shual,\x + \xo 4:28-33: \xt Josh 19:2-8.\x*
\v 29 Bilhah, Etsem, Tolad,
\v 30 Betuel, Hormah, Tsiklag,
\v 31 Beyt-Hamarkavot, Hatsar-Susim, Beyt-Biri, and Sha’arayim. \add They lived in those places until David became king\add*.
\v 32 They \add also\add* lived in five villages: Eytam, Ayin, Rimon, Token, and Ashan,
\v 33 and all the villages around these towns as far as Ba’al. Those were their settlements, and they kept their genealogical records.
\p
\v 34 \add Their clan leaders were\add* Meshovav, Yamlek, Yoshah the son of Amatsyah,
\v 35 Yoel \add (Joel)\add*, Yehu \add (Jehu)\add* the son of Yoshivyah, the son of Serayah, the son of Asiyel,
\v 36 as well as Elyehoeynay, Ya’akovah, Yeshohayah, Asayah, Adiel, Yeshimael, Benayah,
\v 37 and Ziza the son of Shifei, the son of Alon, the son of Yedayah, the son of Shimri, the son of Shemayah.
\v 38 Those who are named above were the leaders of their clans, and their \add extended\add* families increased \add greatly\add* in number,
\v 39 and they went to the entrance of Gedor (to the east of the valley) in search of pasture for their flocks.
\v 40 They found good, \add fertile\add* pasture, and the land was \add wide\add*, and quiet and peaceful, because Ham’s \add descendants\add* had lived there \add previously\add*.
\p
\v 41 Those listed by \add name\add* went \add there\add* in the days of Yehudah’s \add (Judah)\add* King Hizkiyah \add (Hezekiah)\add* and struck down the tents of the Meunim who were found there. They completely annihilated them (to this day), and they lived \add there\add* in their place because there was pasture for their flocks there.
\v 42 Then five hundred of \add their\add* men who were descendants of Shim’on \add (Simeon)\add* went to Mt. Se’ir, \add led\add* by Yishei’s sons Pelatyah, Nearyah, Refayah, and Uziel.
\v 43 They \add also\add* struck down the rest of the Amalekim who escaped, and they’ve lived there to this day.
\c 5
\s1 Re’uven’s descendants
\p
\v 1 Re’uven was the first \add child\add* born to Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* \add (also known as Ya’akov\add* \add (Jacob)\add* \add )\add*, but when he \add had sex with one of his father’s slave-wives\add*, his birthright was given to the sons of Yosef \add (Joseph)\add*, \add another one of Yisrael’s sons\add*. \add So Re’uven wasn’t recorded as inheriting\add* the birthright,
\v 2 \add and although\add* Yehudah \add (Judah)\add* was stronger than his \add other\add* brothers and \add would be the son to produce a ruler\add*, the birthright went to Yosef.\x + \xo 5:1-2: \xt Gen 35:22; 49:3-4; Gen 49:8-10.\x*
\v 3 The sons of Yisrael’s eldest son Re’uven were Hanok, Palu, Hetsron, and Karmi.
\p
\v 4 \add Another of Re’uven’s descendants was\add* Yoel \add (Joel)\add*, and his son was Shemayah. \add Shemayah’s\add* son was Gog. \add Gog’s\add* son was Shimei.
\v 5 \add Shimei’s\add* son was Mikah. \add Mikah’s\add* son was Reayah. \add Reayah’s\add* son was Ba’al.
\v 6 \add Ba’al’s\add* son was Be’erah, who became a leader of the Re’uvenim, \add but\add* the Assyrian King Tiglat-Pileser \add captured him and took him back to Ashur\add* \add (Assyria)\add*.\x + \xo 5:6: \xt 2Ki 15:29.\x*
\v 7 His brothers by their clans, \add as listed in their genealogical records\add*: Yeiel the leader, Zekaryah \add (Zechariah)\add*,
\v 8 Bela son of Azaz, son of Shema, son of Yoel. \add Re’uven’s clan\add* lived near \add the city of\add* Aroer \add as far north as the cities of\add* Nevo and Ba’al-Me’on.
\v 9 \add Some of them lived further\add* east, as far as the edge of the desert that extends to the Perat \add (Euphrates)\add* River, because their livestock had multiplied \add back\add* in the Gilead region.
\v 10 (In the days of \add King\add* Sha’ul \add (Saul)\add*, they’d battled with the Hagrim and \add defeated them, so then\add* they’d lived in their tents all over \add eastern\add* Gilead.)
\s1 Gad’s descendants
\p
\v 11 Gad’s descendants lived opposite them in the Bashan region as far as Salkah:
\v 12 Yoel \add (Joel)\add* was \add their\add* leader, Shafam was second-in-command, others were Ya’anay and Shafat in Bashan.
\v 13 \add Their cousins who were leaders of seven clans were\add* Mika’el \add (Michael)\add*, Meshulam, Sheva, Yoray, Ya’akan, Ziea, and Ever.
\v 14 Those were descendants of Avihayil, the son of Huri, the son of Yaroah, the son of Gilead, the son of Mika’el, the son of Yeshishay, the son of Yahdo, the son of Buz.
\v 15 Ahi was the son of Avdiel, the son of Guni, head of their clans.
\v 16 \add Those descendants of Gad\add* lived in \add towns\add* in the Gilead and Bashan \add regions\add*, and on all the pastureland \add throughout the Sharon Plain\add*.
\v 17 All of them were listed in the genealogies in the days of Yehudah’s \add (Judah)\add* King Yotam \add (Jotham)\add* and Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* King Yarov’am \add (Jeroboam)\add*.
\s1 The eastern warriors
\p
\v 18 There were 44,760 warriors descended from \add strong\add* men in the tribes of Re’uven and Gad and the \add eastern\add* half of the tribe of Menasheh \add (Manasseh)\add*. They all carried \add shields\add* and \add swords\add*, and \add bows\add* and arrows, and were all trained to fight in battles.
\v 19 They went to war against the Hagrim, Yetur, Nafish, and Nodav,
\v 20 and were helped because they called out to God in the battle. \add So\add* with God answering their prayers because of their trust in him, they \add were able to defeat\add* the Hagrim and their \add allies\add*.
\v 21 They captured their livestock, including 50,000 camels, 250,000 sheep, and 2,000 donkeys, as well as 100,000 people,
\v 22 because many were killed \add as a result of God’s assistance\add*. Then they lived in that \add area\add* until the exile.
\s1 Menasheh’s descendants
\p
\v 23 The descendants of the half tribe of Menasheh \add (Manasseh)\add* lived in the Bashan region as far as Ba’al-Hermon and Senir, and Mt. Hermon, \add and\add* they \add increased in number\add*.
\v 24 Their clan leaders were Efer, Yishei, Eliel, Azriel, Yirmiyah \add (Jeremiah)\add*, Hodavyah, and Yahdiyel. They were all powerful and famous warriors, and leaders of their clans.
\s1 The exile of the eastern tribes
\p
\v 25 \add But\add* they disobeyed their ancestors’ god, and they prostituted themselves to the gods of the peoples that God had destroyed \add ahead of them\add* in that region.
\v 26 \add So\add* Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* god stirred up the spirit of the Assyrian King Pul\x + \xo 5:26: \xt 2Ki 15:19.\x* (\add also known as\add* Tiglat-Pileser).\x + \xo 5:26: \xt 2Ki 15:29.\x* He took them into exile—that is, the Re’uvenim, the Gadim, and the half tribe of Menasheh \add (Manasseh)\add*. Then he took them to Halah, Havor, Hara, and the river of Gozan (\add where they still are\add* to this day).\x + \xo 5:26: \xt 2Ki 17:6.\x*
\c 6
\s1 Levi’s descendants
\p
\v 1 Levi’s sons were Gereshon, Kohat, and Merari.
\p
\v 2 Kohat’s sons were Amram, Yitshar, Hevron, and Uziel.
\p
\v 3 Amram’s children were Miryam \add (Miriam)\add*, Aharon \add (Aaron)\add*, and Mosheh \add (Moses)\add*.
\p Aharon’s sons were Nadav, Avihu, Eleazar, and Itamar.
\p
\v 4 Eleazar was the father of Pinhas, \add and\add* Pinhas was the father of Avishua.
\v 5 Avishua was the father of Buki, and Buki was the father of Uzi.
\v 6 Uzi was the father of Zerahyah, and Zerahyah was the father of Merayot.
\v 7 Merayot fathered Amaryah, and Amaryah fathered Ahituv.
\v 8 Ahituv was the father of Tsadok, and Tsadok was the father of Ahima’ats.
\v 9 Ahima’ats was the father of Azaryah, and Azaryah was the father of Yohanan.
\v 10 Yohanan was the father of Azaryah. \add Azaryah\add* was a priest in the temple that Shelomoh \add (Solomon)\add* built in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*.
\v 11 Azaryah was the father of Amaryah, and Amaryah was the father of Ahituv.
\v 12 Ahituv was the father of Tsadok, and Tsadok was the father of Shalum.
\v 13 Shalum was the father of Hilkiyah, and Hilkiyah was the father of Azaryah.
\v 14 Azaryah was the father of Serayah, and Serayah was the father of Yehotsadak.
\p
\v 15 Yehotsadak \add was included\add* when Yahweh \add sent\add* Nevukadnetsar \add (Nebuchadnezzar)\add* to take Yehudah \add (Judah)\add* (\add which includes\add* Yerushalem) into exile.
\s1 Levi’s other descendants
\p
\v 16 Levi’s sons were Gereshon, Kohat, and Merari.\x + \xo 6:16-19: \xt Exo 6:16-19.\x*
\v 17 The names of Gereshon’s sons were Livni and Shimei.
\v 18 Kohat’s sons were Amram, Yitshar, Hevron, and Uziel.
\v 19 Merari’s sons were Mahli and Mushi.
\p \add Here’s a list of\add* \add Levi’s descendants who became leaders of their clans:\add*
\p
\v 20 Gereshon’s \add oldest\add* son was Livni, \add Livni’s\add* son was Yahat, \add and\add* \add Yahat’s\add* son was Zimah.
\v 21 \add Zimah’s\add* son was Yoah, \add Yoah’s\add* son was Ido, \add Ido’s\add* son was Zerah, \add and\add* \add Zerah’s\add* son was Ye’eteray.
\p
\v 22 \add Another line of Kohat’s descendants started with his son Aminadav.\add* Aminadav’s son was Korah. \add Korah’s\add* son was Asir.
\v 23 \add Asir’s\add* son was Elkanah. \add Elkanah’s\add* son was Evyasaf. \add Evyasaf’s\add* son was Asir.
\v 24 \add Asir’s\add* \add son was Tahat.\add* Tahat’s son was Uriel. \add Uriel’s\add* son was Uziyah. \add Uziyah’s\add* son was Shaul.
\p
\v 25 Elkanah’s sons were Amasay, Ahimot,
\v 26 and a son also named Elkanah. Elkanah’s son was Tsuf. Tsuf’s son was Nahat.
\v 27 \add Nahat’s\add* \add son was Eliav.\add* Eliav’s son was Yeroham. \add Yeroham’s\add* son was Elkanah.
\p
\v 28 Shmuel \add (Samuel)\add* had sons: his oldest son \add Yoel\add* \add (Joel)\add* and his other \add son\add* Aviyah.
\p
\v 29 Merari’s descendants started with Mahli. \add Mahli’s\add* son was Livni. \add Livni’s\add* son was Shimei. \add Shimei’s\add* son was Uza.
\v 30 \add Uza’s\add* \add son was Shimea.\add* Shimea’s son was Hagiyah. \add Hagiyah’s\add* son was Asayah.
\s1 The temple musicians
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 31 These \add are the men that\add* David \add put in charge of\add* music in Yahweh’s residence, after the \add sacred\add* box was placed there.
\v 32 They were the ones who’d ministered in the \add sacred tent\add* (\add also called\add* ‘the meeting tent’) with music until Shelomoh \add (Solomon)\add* built the temple in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*. They performed their duties according to their instructions.
\p
\v 33 \add Here’s a list of the musicians\add* and their sons:
\li1 From Kohat’s descendants there was Heyman, the leader of the singers.
\li1 Heyman was Yoel’s \add (Joel)\add* son.
\li1 Yoel was Shmuel’s \add (Samuel)\add* son.
\li1
\v 34 Shmuel was Elkanah’s son.
\li1 Elkanah was Yeroham’s son.
\li1 Yeroham was Eliel’s son.
\li1 Eliel was Toah’s son.
\li1
\v 35 Toah was Tsuf’s son.
\li1 Tsuf was Elkanah’s son.
\li1 Elkanah was Mahat’s son.
\li1 Mahat was Amasay’s son.
\li1
\v 36 Amasay was Elkanah’s son.
\li1 Elkanah was Yoel’s \add (Joel)\add* son.
\li1 Yoel was Azaryah’s son.
\li1 Azaryah was Tsefanyah’s \add (Zephaniah)\add* son.
\li1
\v 37 Tsefanyah was Tahat’s son.
\li1 Tahat was Asir’s son.
\li1 Asir was Evyasaf’s son.
\li1 Evyasaf was Korah’s son.
\li1
\v 38 Korah was Yitshar’s son.
\li1 Yitshar was Kohat’s son.
\li1 Kohat was Levi’s son.
\li1 Levi was the son of \add Ya’akov\add* \add (Jacob)\add* \add (or Ya’akov, who was later named\add* Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*/\add Yisrael)\add*.
\p
\v 39 \add Heyman’s\add* cousin Asaf’s \add group\add* stood to the right. Asaf was Berekyah’s son. Berekyah was Shimea’s son.
\v 40 Shimea was Mika’el’s \add (Michael)\add* son. Mika’el was Ba’aseyah’s son. Ba’aseyah was Malkiyah’s son.
\v 41 Malkiyah was Etni’s son. Etni was Zerah’s son. Zerah was Adayah’s son.
\v 42 Adayah was Eytan’s son. Eytan was Zimah’s son. Zimah was Shimei’s son.
\v 43 Shimei was Yahat’s son. Yahat was Gereshon’s son. Gereshon was Levi’s son.
\p
\v 44 \add Heyman’s\add* cousin Merari’s \add group\add* stood to the left. Eytan was Kishi’s son. Kishi was Avdi’s son. Avdi was Maluk’s son.
\v 45 Maluk was Hashavyah’s son. Hashavyah was Uziyah’s son. Uziyah was Hilkiyah’s son.
\v 46 Hilkiyah was Amtsi’s son. Amtsi was Bani’s son. Bani was Shemer’s son.
\v 47 Shemer was Mahli’s son. Mahli was Mushi’s son. Mushi was Merari’s son. Merari was Levi’s son.
\p
\v 48 Their other Levite cousins were assigned to various ministries serving in God’s residence in the \add sacred tent\add*.
\v 49 \add It was\add* Aharon \add (Aaron)\add* and his sons who \add were responsible for offering\add* the sacrifices on the altar for burnt offerings and on the altar for incense, for all the work of the holiest place, and for making atonement for Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*, according to everything that God’s servant Mosheh \add (Moses)\add* had instructed.
\v 50 These were Aharon’s descendants: \add Aharon’s\add* son was Eleazar. \add Eleazar’s\add* son was Pinhas. \add Pinhas’s\add* son was Avishua.
\v 51 Avishua’s son was Buki. \add Buki’s\add* son was Uzi. \add Uzi’s\add* son was Zerahyah.
\v 52 \add Zerahyah’s\add* son was Merayot. \add Merayot’s\add* son was Amaryah. \add Amaryah’s\add* son was Ahituv.
\v 53 \add Ahituv’s\add* son was Tsadok, and \add Tsadok’s\add* son was Ahima’ats.
\s1 The Levites’ settlements
\p
\v 54 \add Here’s a list of places where Aharon’s\add* \add (Aaron)\add* \add descendants lived. Kohat’s descendants were the first to be allotted property\add*.
\v 55 They were given \add the city of\add* Hevron in Yehudah \add (Judah)\add* and the surrounding pastureland,
\v 56 \add except that\add* Kalev \add (Caleb)\add* received the \add fields\add* \add farther from\add* the city and the villages \add around\add* the city. (Kalev was Yefuneh’s son.)
\v 57 Those descendants of Aharon were given Hevron (a refuge city), as well as these towns and their \add nearby\add* pastureland: Livnah, Yatir, Eshtemoa,
\v 58 Hilen, Devir,
\v 59 Ashan, and Beyt-Shemesh \add (Heliopolis)\add*.
\v 60 From the Benyamin tribe, they received the cities of Geva, Alemet, and Anatot and their pasturelands. Altogether \add those Kohatite\add* clans received thirteen cities,
\v 61 and the remaining Kohatite clans received ten cities from the clans of the Menasheh \add (Manasseh)\add* tribe.
\p
\v 62 The clans of Gereshon’s descendants received thirteen cities from the tribes of Yisakar \add (Issachar)\add*, Asher, Naftali \add (Naphtali)\add*, and the part of the Menasheh tribe that lived in the Bashan \add region\add* \add across the Yarden\add* \add (Jordan)\add* \add River\add*.
\v 63 The clans of Merari’s descendants \add received\add* twelve cities from the tribes of Re’uven, Gad, and Zevulun.
\v 64 \add The Israeli leaders allotted those\add* towns and the nearby pasturelands to the Levites.
\v 65 They also allotted them the cities from the tribes of Yehudah \add (Judah)\add*, Shim’on \add (Simeon)\add*, and Benyamin that were listed above.
\v 66 Some of Kohat’s descendants received cities from the Efrayim \add (Ephraim)\add* tribe.
\v 67 They \add also\add* gave them these cities with the surrounding pasturelands: Shekem (a refuge city), Gezer,
\v 68 Yakemeam, Beyt-Horon,
\v 69 Ayalon, and Gat-Rimon.
\v 70 Kohat’s other descendants received \add the cities of\add* Aner and Bil’am \add (Balaam)\add* and the nearby pastureland from the half-tribe of Menasheh \add (that lives west of the Yarden River)\add*.
\p
\v 71 Gereshon’s descendants also received, from the part of the Menasheh tribe \add that lived across the Yarden River\add*, \add two cities\add* and pastureland near them: Golan in the Bashan \add region\add*, and Ashtarot.
\p
\v 72 From the Yisakar \add (Issachar)\add* \add tribe they received these cities and\add* their nearby pastureland: Kedesh, Davrat,
\v 73 Ramot, and Anem.
\v 74 From the Asher tribe \add they received these cities and\add* their nearby pastureland: Mashal, Avdon,
\v 75 Hukok, and Rehov.
\p
\v 76 From the Naftali \add (Naphtali)\add* tribe \add they received these cities and\add* their nearby pastureland: Kedesh (in the Galil \add region\add*), Hamon, and Kiryatayim.
\p
\v 77 Merari’s descendants \add received these cities and\add* their nearby pasturelands from the Zevulun tribe: Rimon and Tavor.
\p
\v 78 From the Re’uven tribe across the Yarden \add (Jordan)\add* \add River\add* (opposite Yeriho \add (Jericho)\add*), \add they were allotted these cities and towns\add* and their nearby pastureland: Betser in the wilderness, Yahats,
\v 79 Kedemot, and Mofa’at.
\p
\v 80 From the Gad tribe, \add they received these cities and\add* their nearby pastureland: Ramot in the Gilead \add region\add*, Mahanayim,
\v 81 Heshbon, and Yazer.
\c 7
\s1 The descendants of Yisakar (Issachar)
\p
\v 1 Yisakar’s \add (Issachar)\add* four sons were Tola, Puah, Yashuv, and Shimron.
\v 2 Tola’s sons were Uzi, Refayah, Yeriel, Yahmay, Yivsam, and Shmuel \add (Samuel)\add*, \add who all became\add* leaders of their clans. \add There were strong warriors in the record of Tola’s descendants in each generation\add*. (During the time when David was Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* king, there were 22,600 such men.)
\p
\v 3 Uzi’s son was Yizrahyah. Yizrahyah’s five sons were Mika’el \add (Michael)\add*, Ovadyah \add (Obadiah)\add*, Yoel \add (Joel)\add*, and Yishiyah. They were all \add leaders of their clans\add*.
\v 4 Along with them, according to their \add family records\add*, were 36,000 troops in the army, because they \add had numerous\add* wives and sons.
\v 5 Altogether, Yisakar’s clans had 87,000 \add strong\add* warriors listed in their genealogy.
\s1 Benyamin’s descendants
\p
\v 6 Benyamin’s three \add sons\add* were Bela, Beker, and Yedieael.
\v 7 Bela’s five sons were Etsbon, Uzi, Uziel, Yerimot, and Iri, who were leaders of their clans. According to their family records, they had 22,034 \add strong\add* warriors.
\p
\v 8 Beker’s sons were Zemirah, Yoash, Eliezer, Elyehoeynay, Omri, Yerimot, Aviyah, Anatot, and Alemet.
\v 9 Altogether, their clans had 20,200 \add strong\add* warriors listed in their genealogy.
\p
\v 10 Yedieael’s son was Bilhan. Bilhan’s sons were Ye’ush, Benyamin, Ehud, Kena’anah, Zeytan, Tarshish, and Ahishahar.
\v 11 All those descendants of Yedieael were \add clan leaders\add* and \add strong\add* warriors, having 17,200 men ready to go to war.
\p
\v 12 The Shupim and Hupim were descendants of Ir, \add and\add* the Hushim were descendants of Aher.
\s1 The descendants of Naftali (Naphtali)
\p
\v 13 Naftali’s \add (Naphtali)\add* sons were Yahtsiel, Guni, Yetser, and Shalum. (\add They were\add* descendants of \add Ya’akov’s\add* \add (Jacob)\add* \add slave-wife\add* Bilhah.)
\s1 Menasheh’s descendants
\p
\v 14 Menasheh \add (Manasseh)\add* had sons by his \add Arami\add* \add (Syrian)\add* (Heb. Aramean) slave-wife. She gave birth to Asriel and Makir (\add who fathered\add* Gilead).
\v 15 Makir \add married\add* a wife from the Hupim and Shupim. (His sister’s name was Ma’akah.) \add Another\add* descendant was Tselofehad, and he had daughters.
\v 16 Makir’s wife (\add also called\add* Ma’akah) bore a son and named him Peresh (and named his brother Sheresh) and his sons were Ulam and Rekem.
\v 17 Ulam’s son was Bedan. Those were the descendants of Gilead (Makir’s son and Menasheh’s grandson).
\p
\v 18 \add Gilead’s\add* sister was Hamoleket. She was the mother of Ishehod, Aviezer, and Mahlah.
\p
\v 19 \add Another of Gilead’s sons was\add* Shemida, whose sons were Ahyan, Shekem, Likhi, and Anieam.
\s1 Efrayim’s descendants
\p
\v 20 These are Efrayim’s \add (Ephraim)\add* descendants:
\p One of Efrayim’s sons was Shutelah. \add Shutelah’s\add* son was Bered. \add Bered’s\add* son was Tahat. \add Tahat’s\add* son was Eleadah. \add Eleadah’s\add* son \add was also named\add* Tahat.
\v 21 \add Tahat’s\add* son was Zavad. \add Zavad’s\add* son was Shutelah. Efrayim’s other sons, Ezer and Elead, went to Gat \add city\add* to steal some cattle but were killed by \add some of the natives of that area\add*.
\v 22 Their father Efrayim mourned for \add quite some time\add*, and his relatives came to comfort him.
\v 23 Then he \add slept with\add* his wife and she \add got pregnant\add* and \add gave birth to\add* a son. \add Efrayim\add* \add named him\add* Beriah \add (which sounds like the Hebrew word for ‘trouble’)\add*, because \add of the recent tragedy in their family\add*.
\p
\v 24 \add Efrayim’s\add* daughter was She’erah and she founded \add three towns\add*: Lower Beyt-Horon, Upper Beyt-Horon, and Uzen-She’erah.
\p
\v 25 \add Another of Efrayim’s sons\add* was Refah. Refah’s son was Reshef. \add Reshef’s\add* son was Telah. \add Telah’s\add* son was Tahan.
\v 26 \add Tahan’s\add* son was Laedan. \add Laedan’s\add* son was Amihud. \add Amihud’s\add* son was Elishama.
\v 27 \add Elishama’s\add* son was Nun. \add Nun’s\add* son was Yehoshua \add (Joshua)\add* \add (or Yehoshua, who led Yisrael’s\add* \add (Israel)\add* \add descendants after Mosheh\add* \add (Moses)\add* \add died)\add*.
\p
\v 28 \add This is a list of\add* \add the cities and areas where Efrayim’s descendants lived\add*: Beyt-El \add (Bethel)\add* and the nearby villages, Na’aran to the east, Gezer to the west and the nearby villages, and Shekem and the nearby villages extending \add north\add* as far as Ayah and its villages.
\p
\v 29 Along the border of the area where Menasheh’s \add (Manasseh)\add* descendants lived were these towns: Beyt-Shean, Ta’anak, Megidon, Dor, and all the nearby villages. The people who lived in all those places were descendants of Ya’akov’s \add (Jacob)\add* son Yosef \add (Joseph)\add*.
\s1 Asher’s descendants
\p
\v 30 Asher’s sons were Yimnah, Yishvah, Yishvi, and Beriah. Their sister was Serah.
\p
\v 31 Beriah’s sons were Hever and Malkiel. \add Malkiel\add* was the father of Birzot.
\p
\v 32 Hever was the father of Yaflet, Shomer, Hotam, and their sister Shua.
\v 33 Yaflet’s sons were Pasak, Bimhal, and Ashvat.
\p
\v 34 Shomer’s sons were Ahi, Rohagah, Yehubah, and Aram.
\p
\v 35 \add Shomer’s younger\add* brother was Helem. Helem’s sons were Tsofah, Yimna, Shelesh, and Amal.
\p
\v 36 Tsofah’s sons were Suah, Harnefer, Shual, Beri, Yimrah,
\v 37 Betser, Hod, Shama, Shilshah, Yitran (\add whose other name was Yeter\add*), and Be’era.
\p
\v 38 Yeter’s sons were Yefuneh, Pispah, and Ara.
\v 39 \add Another of Asher’s descendants was\add* Ula, whose sons were Arah, Haniyel, and Ritsya.
\p
\v 40 All those were Asher’s descendants, \add clan leaders\add*, chosen, very \add strong\add*, \add leaders of leaders\add*. And there were 26,000 men listed in their genealogy in the army.
\c 8
\s1 Benyamin’s descendants
\p
\v 1 Benyamin had sons: Bela, Ashbel, Ahrah,
\v 2 Nohah, and the fifth, Rafa.
\p
\v 3 Bela’s sons were Adar, Gera, Avihud,
\v 4 Avishua, Na’aman, Ahoah,
\v 5 Gera, Shefufan, and Huram.
\p
\v 6 \add One of Gera’s sons was\add* Ehud. \add His descendants were leaders of their clans who lived\add* in Geva \add city\add*, but were taken into exile to Manahat.
\v 7 Ehud’s sons were Na’aman, Ahiyah, and Gera. \add Gera\add* \add was the one who led them\add* \add when they moved to Manahat\add*. He \add was the father of\add* Uza and Ahihud.
\p
\v 8 \add Another of Benyamin’s\add* \add descendants was\add* Shaharayim, \add who lived\add* in the Moav \add (Moab)\add* countryside. He \add divorced\add* his wives Hushim and Ba’ara,
\v 9 and fathered \add sons\add* with his wife Hodesh: Yovav, Tsivya, Meysha, Malkam,
\v 10 Ye’uts, Shaveyah, and Mirmah. They all became leaders of their clans.
\v 11 (He’d fathered Avituv and Elpa’al by Hushim.)
\p
\v 12 Elpa’al’s sons were Ever, Misham, Shemer (who built Ono \add city\add*, plus Lod and its towns),
\s2 Benyamin’s descendants in Gat and Ayalon
\p
\v 13 plus Beriah and Shema, who were leaders of their clans, who lived in Ayalon \add city\add*. They forced the people who lived in Gat \add city\add* to flee.
\v 14-16 Beriah’s sons were Ahyo, Shashak, Yerimot, Zevadyah, Arad, Eder, Mika’el \add (Michael)\add*, Yishpah, and Yoha.
\s2 Benyamin’s descendants in Yerushalem
\p
\v 17-18 Elpa’al’s \add other\add* descendants included Zevadyah, Meshulam, Hizki, Hever, Yishmeray, Yizliah, and Yovav.
\p
\v 19-21 \add Another of Benyamin’s\add* \add descendants was Shimei.\add* Shimei’s descendants \add included\add* Yakim, Zikri, Zavdi, Eli’eynay, Tsiletay, Eliel, Adayah, Berayah, and Shimrat.
\p
\v 22-25 Shashak’s sons were Yishpan, Ever, Eliel, Avdon, Zikri, Hanan, Hananyah, Eylam, Antotiyah, Yifdeyah, and Penuel.
\p
\v 26-27 \add Another of Benyamin’s descendants was\add* Yeroham, whose sons were Shamsheray, Sheharyah, Atalyah \add (Athaliah)\add*, Ya’areshyah, Eliyah \add (Elijah)\add*, and Zikri.
\p
\v 28 \add All those descendants of Elpa’al (Shaharayim’s son)\add* were \add leaders of their clans according to their genealogies\add*. They \add all\add* lived in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*.
\s2 Benyamin’s descendants in Giveon
\p
\v 29 \add Another of Benyamin’s\add* \add descendants was Yeiel\add*—he \add was a founder of\add* Giveon \add city\add*. His wife was Ma’akah.
\v 30 His sons, \add starting with\add* the eldest, were Avdon, Tsur, Kish, Ba’al, Nadav,
\v 31 Gedor, Ahyo, and Zeker.
\v 32 Miklot \add (another of Yeiel’s sons)\add* was Shimeah’s father. All those descendants of Yeiel also lived in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* near their relatives.
\s1 The descendants of King Sha’ul (Saul)
\p
\v 33 Ner was the father of Kish. Kish was the father of \add King\add* Sha’ul \add (Saul)\add*. Sha’ul was the father of Yonatan \add (Jonathan)\add*, Malkishua, Avinadav, and Eshba’al.
\v 34 Yonatan’s son was Meriv-Ba’al. Meriv-Ba’al was the father of Mikah.
\p
\v 35 Mikah’s sons were Piton, Melek, Ta’area, and Ahaz.
\v 36 Ahaz was the father of Yeho’adah. Yeho’adah was the father of Alemet, Azmavet, and Zimri. Zimri was the father of Motsa.
\v 37 Motsa was the father of Binea. Rafa was \add Binea’s\add* son. Eleasah was Rafa’s son. Atsel was \add Eleasah’s\add* son.
\p
\v 38 Atsel had \add a total of\add* six sons, named Azrikam, Bokru, Yishmael \add (Ishmael)\add*, Shearyah, Ovadyah \add (Obadiah)\add*, and Hanan.
\v 39 \add Atsel\add* had a brother, Eshek. Eshek’s oldest son was Ulam. His other \add sons\add* were Ye’ush and Elifelet.
\p
\v 40 Ulam’s sons were powerful warriors who were also archers. Altogether they had 150 sons and grandsons.
\p All those were Benyamin’s descendants.
\c 9
\s1 Those who returned from captivity
\p
\v 1 \add So the genealogy of all Israelis was listed\add*—yes, written on the scroll of Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* kings.
\p Then Yehudah \add (Judah)\add* was taken into exile to Bavel \add (Babylon)\add* because of their unfaithfulness.
\v 2 The first \add to return and resettle their land and\add* their cities were \add some\add* priests, Levites, temple servants, and \add other\add* Israelis.\x + \xo 9:2-3: \xt Ezr 2:70; Neh 7:73.\x*
\v 3 \add Some from the tribes of\add* Yehudah, Benyamin, Efrayim \add (Ephraim)\add*, and Menasheh \add (Manasseh)\add* \add also returned and\add* lived in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*, \add including the following:\add*
\p
\v 4 Utay son of Amihud (Amihud was Omri’s son, Omri was Imri’s son, Imri was Bani’s son, Bani was a \add descendant\add* of Perets, Perets was Yehudah’s son),
\v 5 Asayah and his sons (descendants of Shilon—Asayah was the oldest),
\v 6 and Ye’uel and 690 other relatives from Zerah’s clan.
\p
\v 7 From Benyamin’s descendants, Salu (who was Meshulam’s son, Meshulam was Hodavyah’s son, Hodavyah was Senuah’s son),
\v 8 Yivneyah (Yeroham’s son), Elah (who was Uzi’s son, Uzi was Mikri’s son), and Meshulam (who was Shefatyah’s son, Shefatyah was Re’uel’s son, Re’uel was Yivniyah’s son).
\p
\v 9 In all, there were 956 of Benyamin’s descendants living in Yerushalem. All those \add above\add* were leaders of their clans.
\p
\v 10 Some of the priests \add who returned to Yehudah\add* \add (Judah)\add* were: Yedayah, Yehoyariv, Yakin,
\v 11 Azaryah, the leading official at the temple (Azaryah was Hilkiyah’s son, Hilkiyah was Meshulam’s son, Meshulam was Tsadok’s son, Tsadok was Merayot’s son, Merayot was Ahituv’s son),
\v 12 Adayah (who was Yeroham’s son, Yeroham was Pashhur’s son, Pashhur was Malkiyah’s son), and Ma’asay (who was Adiel’s son, Adiel was Yahzerah’s son, Yahzerah was Meshulam’s son, Meshulam was Meshilemit’s son, Meshilemit was Imer’s son).
\p
\v 13 Altogether there were 1,760 \add priests who returned to Yehudah\add*. \add They were leaders of their clans, and they were all capable and responsible\add* to work in God’s residence.
\s1 Levites who returned
\p
\v 14 From Levi’s descendants \add who returned to Yehudah\add* \add (Judah)\add* \add there were\add*: Shemayah (who was Hashuv’s son, Hashuv was Azrikam’s son, Azrikam was Hashavyah’s son, Hashavyah was a descendant of \add Levi’s youngest son\add* Merari),
\v 15 Bakbakar, Heresh, Galal, Matanyah (who was Mika’s son, Mika was Zikri’s son, Zikri was Asaf’s son),
\v 16 Ovadyah \add (Obadiah)\add* (who was Shemayah’s son, Shemayah was Galal’s son, Galal was Yedutun’s son), and Berekyah who lived in one of the villages where the Netofat people lived (Berekyah was Asa’s son, Asa was Elkanah’s son).
\p
\v 17 \add From Levi’s descendants who returned to Yehudah and\add* who guarded the \add temple\add* gates there were Shalum, Akuv, Talmon, Ahiman, and some of their relatives. Shalum was \add their\add* leader.
\v 18 Those descendents of Levi were gatekeepers at the King’s Gate on the east, \add and their descendants still are, even to this day\add*.
\p
\v 19 Shalum was Kore’s son, Kore was Evyasaf’s son, and Evyasaf was Korah’s son. Shalum and his relatives from his clan (Korah’s descendants) had the duty of being gatekeepers, responsible for guarding the \add entrances\add* to Yahweh’s temple, as their ancestors had done.
\v 20 \add Previously\add* Pinhas (Eleazar’s son) \add had supervised\add* the gatekeepers. Yahweh was with \add Pinhas\add*.
\p
\v 21 Zekaryah \add (Zechariah)\add* (Meshelemyah’s son) was gatekeeper of the entrance to the assembly tent.
\v 22 \add Altogether, there were 212 men chosen to guard the gates\add*. \add The records of the clans in their villages listed their names\add*. \add King\add* David and \add the prophet\add* Shmuel \add (Samuel)\add* \add appointed them because they were dependable\add*.
\v 23 They and their sons \add watched\add* over the gates of Yahweh’s house, that is, the \add sacred\add* tent, as guards.
\v 24 There were gatekeepers on each of the \add temple’s\add* four sides: east, west, north, and south.
\v 25 Their relatives in their villages would come in from time to time \add to relieve them\add* for seven days,
\v 26 because the four leaders of the gatekeepers were Levites, and they were \add also\add* \add trusted to supervise\add* the rooms and the treasuries in God’s residence.
\v 27 They would \add spend the night\add* around the \add temple\add* while they were in charge, and \add would open it with the key each\add* morning.
\p
\v 28 Some of them \add monitored the utensils used in the activities\add*—counting them as they brought them out and \add when they put them away\add* \add again\add*.
\v 29 \add Others\add* \add took care of\add* the containers and utensils used in the sanctuary, along with the fine flour, the wine and oil, and the frankincense and spices.
\v 30 Some of the priests mixed \add the fragrances and\add* spices.
\p
\v 31 There was a Levite \add named\add* Matityah (Shalum’s oldest son, who was a descendant of Korah) whose official duty \add was supervising the baking of the flat breads\add*.
\v 32 Some of Kohat’s descendants prepared the \add rows\add* of bread to \add be laid out on the sacred table\add* every rest day.
\p
\v 33 Some of Levi’s descendants were \add temple musicians and\add* singers. The leaders of those families stayed in the \add temple\add* accommodations. They \add didn’t have other duties,\add* because they \add were expected to\add* serve day and night.
\v 34 Those were the heads of the Levite \add families\add* according to their generations, \add and\add* they lived in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*.
\s1 King Sha’ul’s ancestors and descendants
\r (1 Chr. 8:29-38)
\p
\v 35 \add Another of Benyamin’s\add* \add descendants was\add* Yeiel. He was \add a founder\add* of Giveon \add city\add* and lived there. His wife was Ma’akah.
\v 36 His oldest son was Avdon, and his other \add sons\add* were Tsur, Kish, Ba’al, Ner, Nadav,
\v 37 Gedor, Ahyo, Zekaryah \add (Zechariah)\add*, and Miklot.
\v 38 Miklot was Shim’am’s father. They also lived \add near\add* their relatives in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*.
\p
\v 39 Ner was the father of Kish. Kish was the father of \add King\add* Sha’ul \add (Saul)\add*. Sha’ul was the father of Yonatan \add (Jonathan)\add*, Malkishua, Avinadav, and Eshba’al.
\v 40 Yonatan’s son was Meriv-Ba’al. Meriv-Ba’al was the father of Mikah.
\v 41 Mikah’s sons were Piton, Melek, and Tahrea.
\v 42 Ahaz was the father of Yaerah. Yaerah was the father of Alemet, Azmavet, and Zimri. Zimri was the father of Motsa.
\v 43 Motsa was the father of Binea. Refayah was \add Binea’s\add* son. Eleasah was \add Refayah’s\add* son. Atsel was \add Eleasah’s\add* son.
\p
\v 44 Atsel had six sons, named Azrikam, Bokru, Yishmael \add (Ishmael)\add*, Shearyah, Ovadyah \add (Obadiah)\add*, and Hanan.
\c 10
\s1 The death of King Sha’ul and his sons
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (1 Sam. 31:1-13)
\p
\v 1 \add Now\add* the Pelishtim fought against Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*, and the Israeli \add men\add* fled \add away\add* from the Pelishtim, and \add many were killed\add* on Mt. Gilboa.
\v 2 Then the Pelishtim pursued Sha’ul \add (Saul)\add* and his sons, and \add they\add* \add killed\add* \add his\add* sons Yonatan \add (Jonathan)\add*, Avinadav, and Malkishua.
\v 3 The battle against Sha’ul was very fierce, \add and then\add* the archers spotted him and wounded him.
\v 4 Then Sha’ul said to his equipment carrier, “Draw your sword and pierce me with it, so those uncircumcised \add Pelishtim\add* \add can’t come here\add* and torture me.” But his equipment carrier was very scared and wasn’t willing to do it, so Sha’ul took \add his\add* sword and fell onto it.
\v 5 Then his equipment carrier, seeing that \add King\add* Sha’ul had died, also fell on \add his\add* sword and died.
\v 6 \add So\add* Sha’ul and his three sons died, \add thus ending his family’s rule\add*.
\v 7 \add When all the Israelis living\add* in the valley saw that \add the army\add* had fled, and that Sha’ul and his sons were dead, they abandoned their cities and fled. Then the Pelishtim came, and they lived there \add instead\add*.
\p
\v 8 The next day, \add when\add* the Pelishtim came to strip the dead, they found Sha’ul and his sons’ \add bodies\add* on Mt. Gilboa.
\v 9 They stripped him and carried away his head and his armour. Then they sent \add messengers\add* all around the Pelishti \add regions\add* to take the news to their idols and the people.
\v 10 They put his armour in the \add temple\add* of their gods, and they hung his head in \add their god\add* Dagon’s \add temple\add*.
\v 11 \add When\add* everyone who lived \add back\add* in Yavesh in the Gilead \add region\add* heard about everything the Pelishtim had done to Sha’ul,
\v 12 all the \add strong\add* \add men\add* \add took action\add* and \add retrieved\add* the corpses of Sha’ul and his sons, and brought them \add back\add* to Yavesh. Then they buried \add them\add* under the large tree in Yavesh, and they fasted for seven days.
\v 13 \add So\add* Sha’ul died because of his \add disobedience\add*—when he was unfaithful to Yahweh \add by not following Yahweh’s instructions\add*,\x + \xo 10:13: \xt 1Sam 13:8-14; 15:1-24.\x* and also \add because he consulted a medium\add* \add for guidance\add*\x + \xo 10:13: \xt Lev 19:31; 20:6; 1Sam 28:7-8.\x*
\v 14 \add instead of requesting Yahweh’s advice\add*. \add So\add* \add Yahweh\add* killed him and handed the kingdom over to Yishay’s \add (Jesse)\add* son David.
\c 11
\s1 David becomes king
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 5:1-10)
\p
\v 1 Then all of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* assembled around David at Hevron, saying, “Look, we’re your flesh and \add blood\add* \add relatives\add*.
\v 2 \add In the recent past\add*, even when Sha’ul \add (Saul)\add* was king, \em you\em* were the one who took Yisrael out and the one who brought us back in. Plus your god Yahweh told you, ‘You yourself will shepherd my people, Yisrael, and you yourself will become the leader of my Israeli people.’ ”
\v 3 \add So\add* all the Israeli elders went to the king at Hevron, and David made a solemn agreement with them at Hevron \add in front\add* of Yahweh. Then they anointed David as king over Yisrael in accordance with Yahweh’s message \add that was given through\add* \add the prophet\add* Shmuel \add (Samuel)\add*.
\p
\v 4 Then David and all of Yisrael went to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* (\add Yerushalem, which was also known as\add* Yevus \add because the Yevusim were living there\add*).\x + \xo 11:4: \xt Josh 15:63; Jdg 1:21.\x*
\v 5 The inhabitants \add inside\add* Yevus said to David, “You won’t \add be entering this place\add*.” But David captured \add what became known as\add* the Tsiyon \add (Zion)\add* stronghold, that is, the city of David.
\v 6 \add He’d\add* said, “The one who strikes down the Yevusim first will become \add my army commander\add*.” Tseruyah’s son Yoav \add (Joab)\add* went up \add over the wall\add* first, and he became head \add of the army\add*.
\v 7 David \add started\add* living in that stronghold, so then they called it the city of David.
\v 8 He built the city up all around, from the terrace \add to the surrounding walls\add*, and Yoav restored the rest of the city.
\v 9 \add So\add* David \add became more powerful because\add* Commander-in-chief Yahweh \add helped\add* him.
\s1 David’s powerful warriors
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 10 The following were the leaders of David’s powerful warriors who, together with all of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*, strengthened his kingdom and established him as king, \add just as\add* Yahweh had said.
\v 11 \add So\add* these are David’s powerful warriors: Yashaveam (a Hakmoni \add and\add* the leader of the thirty), who once \add used\add* his spear against three hundred men in a single battle and killed them all.
\v 12 Next was Eleazar (an Ahohi, son of Dodo) who was one of the \add top\add* three powerful warriors.
\v 13 He was with David at Pas-Damim \add when\add* the Pelishtim had assembled there for battle \add near\add* a part of the countryside \add where barley was growing\add*. \add At first, the Israelis\add* fled from the Pelishtim,
\v 14 \add but then\add* they took their stand in the middle of the \add barley\add* and \add proceeded to defeat\add* the Pelishtim \add as\add* Yahweh saved them through a stunning victory.
\p
\v 15 Then three of that top thirty went down to David, near the rock at Adulam’s cave. \add Meanwhile,\add* Pelishtim had encamped in the Refaim Valley.
\v 16 David was in that stronghold then, and the Pelishti garrison was in Beyt-Lehem \add (Bethlehem)\add*,
\v 17 and David \add was thirsty and asked\add*, “Who’ll get me water to drink from the well by the Beyt-Lehem gate?”
\v 18 \add So those\add* three broke through the Pelishti camp, and they drew water from the well at the Beyt-Lehem gate, and they brought it back to David. \add But\add* David wasn’t willing to drink it, and he poured it out \add on the ground as an offering\add* to Yahweh.
\v 19 “Far be it from me \add to drink this water\add* in front of my god,” he said. “Could I drink the blood of these men \add who risked\add* their lives to bring it?” \add So\add* he wasn’t \add prepared\add* to drink it. Those were \add some of the things\add* those three powerful warriors did.
\p
\v 20 Yoav’s \add (Joab)\add* brother Avishay was the head of a group of three. He’d killed three hundred people with his spear and had a \add reputation\add* among the three.\f + \fr 11:20 \ft This seems unexpectedly similar to verse 11 and might possibly indicate some confusion in the early records.\f*
\v 21 He was honoured more than the other two and became the leader of that \add second\add* group of three, \add but\add* didn’t enter the \add first\add* Three.
\p
\v 22 Yehoyada’s son Benayah from Kavtse’el was a powerful warrior who did amazing things. He struck down Ariel’s two \add sons\add* from Moav \add (Moab)\add*. \add Another time,\add* he climbed down into a pit on a snowy day and killed the lion \add that was trapped\add* in the pit.
\v 23 He \add also\add* \add killed\add* a \add huge\add* Mitsri \add (Egyptian)\add* man—well over two metres tall. The Mitsri \add was carrying\add* a spear like a weaver’s beam, but \add Benayah\add* went down to \add take him on\add* with a staff. He forced the spear from the Mitsri’s \add grasp\add*, and then killed him with \add it\add*.
\v 24 \add So\add* that was what Benayah (Yehoyada’s son) did \add that gave him a reputation\add* among the three powerful warriors.
\v 25 \add Actually,\add* he was honoured more than the Thirty, \add yet\add* he didn’t \add make it to\add* the \add first\add* Three, \add but\add* David \add appointed\add* him \add to have authority\add* over his \add bodyguards\add*.
\p
\v 26 These are the names of the powerful warriors: Asahel (Yoav’s brother), Elhanan (Dodo’s son from Beyt-Lehem \add (Bethlehem)\add*),
\v 27 Shamot (from Haror), Helets (from Pelon),
\v 28 Ira (Ikesh’s son from Tekoa), Aviezer (from Anatot),
\v 29 Sibkay (from Hushah), Ilay (from Ahoh),
\v 30 Maharay (from Netofah), Heled (Ba’anah’s son from Netofah),
\v 31 Itay (Rivay’s son from Giveah, which belonged to Benyamin’s descendants), Benayah (from Pireaton),
\v 32 Huray (from the valleys near \add Mt.\add* Ga’ash), Aviel (from Aravah),
\v 33 Azmavet (from Baharum), Elyahba (from Shaalvon),
\v 34 Hashem’s sons (from Gizon), Yonatan \add (Jonathan)\add* (Shageh’s son from Harar),
\v 35 Ahiam (Sakar’s son from Harar), Elifal (Ur’s son),
\v 36 Hefer (from Mekerat), Ahiyah (from Pelon),
\v 37 Hetsro (from Karmel), Na’aray (Ezbay’s son),
\v 38 Yoel \add (Joel)\add* (Natan’s \add (Nathan)\add* brother), Mivhar (Hagri’s son),
\v 39 Tselek (the Amoni), Naharay (from Be’erot, armour bearer for Tseruyah’s son Yoav \add (Joab)\add*),
\v 40 Ira (the Yitri), Garev (the Yitri),
\v 41 Uriyah (\add Bat-Sheva’s\add* \add (Bathsheba)\add* \add husband\add* the Hiti), Zavad (Ahlay’s son),
\v 42 Adina (Shiza’s son from the tribe of Re’uven, a leader from that tribe who had thirty soldiers with him),
\v 43 Hanan (Ma’akah’s son), Yoshafat (from Mithna),
\v 44 Uziya (from Ashterat), Shama and Yeiel (Hotam’s sons from Aroer),
\v 45 Yedieael (Shimri’s son) and his brother Yoha (from Tiz),
\v 46 Eliel (from Mahavah), Yerivay and Yoshavyah (Elna’am’s sons), Yitmah (from Moav \add (Moab)\add*),
\v 47 Eliel and Oved, and Ya’asiyel (from Tsovah).
\c 12
\s1 The heroes accompanied to David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 These were the men who joined David in Tsiklag, when he was \add banished\add* from Kish’s son Sha’ul’s \add (Saul)\add* presence. (They were among the warriors who assisted \add David\add* in battle
\v 2 armed with bows. \add Some\add* \add used slings\add* right-handed and \add some\add* left-handed, as well as \add shooting\add* arrows with a bow. \add They were\add* from \add the\add* Benyamin \add tribe\add*, Sha’ul’s relatives.)
\v 3 \add Their\add* leader was Ahiezer, then next was Yoash, both Shema’ah’s sons from Giveah \add city\add*. \add Others were:\add* Yeziel and Pelet (Azmavet’s sons), Berakah, Yehu \add (Jehu)\add* (from Anatot \add city\add*),
\v 4 Yishmaeyah (\add from Giveon\add* \add city\add*, who \add was the leader of\add* the thirty greatest \add warriors\add*), Yirmiyah \add (Jeremiah)\add*, Yahaziel, Yohanan, Yozavad (\add from Gederah\add* \add city\add*),
\v 5 Eleuzay, Yerimot, Be’alyah, Shemaryah, Shefatyah (from Haruf \add city\add*),
\v 6 Elkanah, Yishiyah, Azarel, Yoezer, and Yashaveam (all Korah’s descendants),
\v 7 and Yoelah and Zevadyah (Yeroham’s sons, from Gedor \add city\add*).
\p
\v 8 \add Some\add* Gadim \add joined\add* David at the wilderness stronghold. \add They were\add* very \add strong\add* army warriors, ready for battle with \add shields\add* and \add spears\add*. \add Their faces were fierce like lions, but they could run across the hills like\add* gazelles.
\v 9 \add Their\add* leader was Ezer, then Ovadyah \add (Obadiah)\add*, then Eliav.
\v 10 Next was Mishmanah, then Yirmiyah,
\v 11 Atay, Eliel,
\v 12 Yohanan, Elzavad,
\v 13 \add another\add* Yirmiyah, then the eleventh was Makbanay.
\p
\v 14 Those men from the Gad \add tribe\add* were army leaders—\add each leading somewhere between\add* a hundred and a thousand \add men\add*.
\v 15 They crossed to the west side of the Yarden \add (Jordan)\add* \add River\add* \add during the spring, at the time of the year when the river floods\add*. They \add drove out all the people who lived in the valleys on both sides\add* \add of the river\add*.
\p
\v 16 \add Some other men\add* \add from the tribes\add* of Benyamin and Yehudah \add (Judah)\add* also went to David in \add his\add* stronghold.
\v 17 David went out \add of the cave\add* \add to meet them\add*, and he told them: “If you’ve come to me in peace, to help me, \add then I’ll be happy to cooperate with\add* you \add men\add*. \add But\add* if \add you men plan\add* to betray me to my enemies \add even though I’ve done you no harm\add*, may the god of our ancestors see \add your motives\add* and judge.”
\p
\v 18 Then \add the\add* spirit \add empowered\add* Amasay, the head of the thirty, \add and he said\add*: “To you, David, and with you, son of Yishay \add (Jesse)\add*: peace. Peace to you and peace to the one who helps you, because your god has helped you.” And David received them and appointed them as the heads of the troop.
\p
\v 19 \add Some men\add* from Menasheh \add (Manasseh)\add* \add joined\add* David when he \add accompanied\add* the Pelishtim to battle against \add King\add* Sha’ul \add (Saul)\add*. (\add But\add* \add David and his men\add* didn’t help \add the Pelishtim\add*, because the Pelishtim’ governors \add were advised\add* to send them away, \add as there was a worry that David would feel loyal to his former master, and hence turn on them\add*.)
\v 20 As \add David\add* was going to Tsiklag, \add the following men from Menasheh joined\add* him: Adnah, Yozavad, Yedieael, Mika’el \add (Michael)\add*, \add another\add* Yozavad, Elihu, and Tsiletay—the \add captains\add* of \add units of\add* thousands from Menasheh.
\v 21 They helped David \add stand\add* against \add bands of raiders\add*, because they were all \add powerful warriors and military leaders\add*.
\v 22 \add Every day more men joined\add* David to help him, until his army became very large, \add like the Israeli people group itself\add*.
\p
\v 23 \add The following are\add* the numbers of the leaders of those equipped for war. They came to David at Hevron to \add help him obtain Sha’ul’s kingdom as Yahweh had promised\add*.
\li1
\v 24 From Yehudah \add (Judah)\add*: 6,800 \add warriors\add* ready for battle with \add shields\add* and \add spears\add*.
\li1
\v 25 From Shim’on \add (Simeon)\add*: 7,100 powerful \add warriors\add* ready for battle.
\li1
\v 26 From Levi: 4,600.
\li1
\v 27 Yehoyada (a leader \add descended\add* from Aharon \add (Aaron)\add*) brought 3,700 with him.
\li1
\v 28 Tsadok, a \add strong\add* young man, \add brought\add* twenty-two leaders \add from his clan\add*.
\li1
\v 29 From Benyamin (Sha’ul’s \add (Saul)\add* tribe): 3,000 (\add most of whom had previously served\add* \add King\add* Sha’ul).
\li1
\v 30 From Efrayim \add (Ephraim)\add*: 20,800 \add powerful warriors who were admired within their own clans\add*.
\li1
\v 31 From the half-tribe of Menasheh \add (Manasseh)\add*: 18,000 who were \add known by name\add* came to make David king.
\li1
\v 32 From Yisakar \add (Issachar)\add*: 200 leaders (\add with a good understanding of the times and the right thing to do\add*) along with their relatives that they commanded.
\li1
\v 33 From Zevulun: 50,000 \add experienced warriors with each kind of weapon\add*, and \add stoic in attitude, yet loyal\add*.
\li1
\v 34 From Naftali \add (Naphtali)\add*: 1,000 officers, along with 37,000 \add men\add* carrying \add shields\add* and \add spears\add*.
\li1
\v 35 From Dan: 28,600 \add men\add* prepared for battle.
\li1
\v 36 From Asher: 40,000 \add warriors\add* prepared for battle.
\li1
\v 37 From Re’uven, Gad, and the Menasheh \add (Manasseh)\add* half-tribe across the Yarden \add (Jordan)\add* \add river\add*: 120,000 \add men\add* armed with all kinds of weapons.
\p
\v 38 All those \add enthusiastic and capable warriors\add* came to Hevron to make David king over all of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*. The rest of Yisrael was also \add united in wanting\add* to make David king.
\v 39 They were there with David three days, eating and drinking, \add because their families had sent provisions with them\add*.
\v 40 Also, people \add who lived near that area\add* (\add even\add* as far as \add the tribal areas belonging to\add* Yisakar \add (Issachar)\add*, Zevulun, and Naftali \add (Naphtali)\add*) were bringing bread on donkeys and camels, and on mules and on oxen: plentiful supplies of flour, fig cakes, raisin cakes, wine, and oil, as well as oxen and sheep, because Yisrael \add was celebrating\add*.
\c 13
\s1 Moving the box containing the agreement
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 6:1-11)
\p
\v 1 \add One day\add* David consulted with all \add his\add* \add leaders\add*—the commanders of thousands and of hundreds.
\v 2 David said to the whole Israeli assembly, “If it \add sounds\add* good to you, and to our god Yahweh, let’s spread out to \add all our different regions to recruit\add* our relatives, along with the priests and the Levites in the cities among their pasturelands, \add so they can join\add* us.
\v 3 And let’s return our god’s box \add back here\add*, because we didn’t \add use it to ask God for guidance in Sha’ul’s\add* \add (Saul)\add* \add time\add*.”
\v 4 All the assembled people agreed because everyone \add could see that it was the right thing to do\add*.
\p
\v 5 \add So\add* David assembled all of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*, from \add everywhere from\add* the Shihor \add river down\add* in Mitsrayim \add (Egypt)\add* to as far \add north\add* as Levo-Hamat, to bring in God’s box from Kiryat-Yearim.\x + \xo 13:5: \xt 1Sam 7:1-2.\x*
\v 6 Then David and all of Yisrael went to Kiryat-Yearim (\add also known as\add* Ba’alah, in Yehudah \add (Judah)\add*), to bring up from there the box of God Yahweh who sits over the winged creatures.\x + \xo 13:6: \xt Exo 25:22.\x* \add The box is used when\add* \add calling his name\add* \add to request help\add*.
\v 7 They transported God’s box on a new cart from Avinadav’s house, and Uza and Ahyo were leading the cart,
\v 8 and David and all of Yisrael were celebrating \add in front\add* of God with all \add their energy\add*, with songs, and with lyres, harps, tambourines, cymbals, and trumpets.
\p
\v 9 \add However,\add* \add just as they were passing\add* Kidon’s threshing floor, the oxen stumbled and Uza \add reached out to steady\add* the box.
\v 10 \add That made\add* Yahweh \add very angry with\add* Uza, and he struck him down because \add he’d reached out to touch\add* the box, and he died \add right\add* there \add in front\add* of God.
\v 11 \add That in turn made\add* David \add angry\add* because Yahweh \add had burst out\add* against Uza, and he named that place Perets-Uza \add (which means ‘outburst against Uza’), which it’s still called,\add* to this day.
\p
\v 12 David \add was afraid of\add* God that day \add and asked himself\add*, “How can I \add possibly\add* bring God’s box to where I am \add in Yerushalem\add* \add (Jerusalem)\add*?”
\v 13 \add So\add* David \add gave up on bringing the box to where he was\add* in the city of David, and \add instead\add* he took it into the \add nearby\add* house belonging to Oved-Edom the Giti.
\v 14 \add So\add* God’s box stayed at Oved-Edom’s house for three months, and Yahweh blessed Oved-Edom’s home and everything that belonged to him.\x + \xo 13:14: \xt 1Ch 26:4-5.\x*
\c 14
\s1 David ‘expansion’ in Yerushalem
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 2:11-16)
\p
\v 1 \add One day\add* King Huram from Tsor \add (Tyre)\add* sent messengers to David, along with cedar timber and \add stone masons and carpenters\add* to build a house for him.
\v 2 \add By then\add* David \add had realised\add* that Yahweh had established him as king over Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*—that his kingdom was \add being made more prominent\add* for the sake of \add Yahweh’s\add* people Yisrael.
\p
\v 3 Then David took more wives in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* and \add he\add* fathered more sons and daughters.
\v 4 These \add are his children\add* who were born \add there\add* in Yerushalem: Shamua, Shovav, Natan \add (Nathan)\add*, Shelomoh \add (Solomon)\add*,
\v 5 Yavehar, Elishua, Elifelet,
\v 6 Nogah, Nefeg, Yafia,
\v 7 Elishama, Be’elyada, and Elifelet.
\s1 The defeat of the Pelishtim
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 5:17-25)
\p
\v 8 \add When\add* the Pelishtim heard that David had been anointed as king over all of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*, the entire Pelishti \add army\add* went up \add into the hill country\add* to \add eliminate\add* \add him\add*. David \add got this news and took his army out to meet them\add*.
\v 9 \add Meanwhile,\add* the Pelishtim went and raided the Refaim valley,
\v 10 and David asked God, “Should I \add take on\add* the Pelishtim, and will you give \add me victory over them\add*?”
\p “Go \add ahead\add*,” Yahweh \add answered\add*, “and I’ll \add enable you to defeat them\add*.”
\p
\v 11 Then they\f + \fr 14:11 \ft It’s not clear who’s being referred to here.\f* went up to \add what’s now called\add* Ba’al-Peratsim, and David \add defeated\add* them down there. David said, “God has \add used me to\add* break through my enemies like \add a flooded river breaking a dam\add*.” Therefore they named that place Ba’al-Peratsim \add (which means ‘the master bursts through’)\add*.
\v 12 \add The Pelishtim\add* abandoned their \add idols\add* \add as they fled\add*, and David ordered them to be burnt.
\p
\v 13 \add But\add* \add soon\add* the Pelishtim raided the valley again,
\v 14 and David asked God’s \add advice\add* again. “Don’t go uphill after them,” God said, “\add but\add* circle around them and confront them in front of the balsam trees.
\v 15 When you hear the sound of the marching in the tops of the balsam trees, \add that’s when you should attack\add*, because God will have gone out \add ahead of you\add* to strike down the Pelishti army.”
\v 16 \add So\add* David did just as God had told him, and he \add attacked\add* the Pelishtim from Giveon \add all the way to\add* Gezer.
\v 17 \add So David’s reputation spread everywhere\add*, and Yahweh \add caused the other nations to be afraid of\add* him.
\c 15
\s1 Moving the box containing the agreement
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add David\add* constructed houses for his \add wives\add* in the city of David. He \add also\add* prepared a place for God’s \add sacred\add* box and erected a tent for it.
\v 2 Then David \add ordered\add*, “Only the Levites are allowed to carry God’s box, because Yahweh chose them to carry the box and to minister for him forever.”\x + \xo 15:2: \xt Deu 10:8.\x*
\v 3 David assembled all of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add* to bring Yahweh’s box uphill there to the place that he’d prepared for it.
\v 4 Then \add he\add* summoned Aharon’s \add (Aaron)\add* descendants and these Levites:
\li1
\v 5 From Kohat’s descendants \add (Levi’s second son)\add*: Uriel the leader and 120 of his relatives.
\li1
\v 6 From Merari’s descendants \add (Levi’s third son)\add*: Asayah the leader and 220 of his relatives.
\li1
\v 7 From Gereshon’s descendants \add (Levi’s eldest son)\add*: Yoel \add (Joel)\add* the leader and 130 of his relatives.
\li1
\v 8 From Elitsafan’s descendants: Shemayah the leader and 200 of his relatives.
\li1
\v 9 From Hevron’s descendants: Eliel the leader and eighty of his relatives.
\li1
\v 10 From Uziel’s descendants: Aminadav the leader and 112 of his relatives.
\p
\v 11 David summoned the priests Tsadok and Evyatar and these Levites: Uriel, Asayah, Yoel, Shemayah, Eliel, and Aminadav.
\v 12 He told them, “You are the heads of the Levite clans. Consecrate yourselves, you and your relatives, and bring Yisrael’s god Yahweh’s box up to where I’ve prepared \add a place\add* for it.
\v 13 The first \add time we tried,\add* it wasn’t you \add Levites, so\add* our god Yahweh burst out against us because we \add hadn’t requested his instruction\add*.”
\p
\v 14 \add So\add* the priests and the Levites consecrated themselves to bring up Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* god Yahweh’s \add sacred\add* box.
\v 15 Then, \add following Yahweh’s instructions as given to Mosheh\add* \add (Moses)\add*, Levi’s descendants carried God’s \add sacred\add* box with its poles on their \add shoulders\add*.\x + \xo 15:15: \xt Exo 25:14.\x*
\p
\v 16 \add Meanwhile,\add* David instructed the Levite leaders to assign their brothers as the singers, with \add accompanying\add* instruments—harps, lyres, and cymbals—playing to raise a sound of \add celebration\add*.
\v 17 The Levites assigned Yoel’s son Heyman and his relative Asaf (son of Berekyah) and Eytan (Kushayah’s son, a descendant of Merari),
\v 18 along with their \add Levite\add* cousins: Zekaryah \add (Zechariah)\add*, Ben, Ya’aziel, Shemiramot, Yehiel, Uni, Eliav, Benayah, Ma’aseyah, Matityah, Elifelehu, Mikneyah, and two of the \add temple\add* gatekeepers, Oved-Edom and Yeiel.
\v 19 Heyman, Asaf, and Eytan \add sang and also played\add* bronze cymbals.
\v 20 Zekaryah, Aziel, Shemiramot, Yehiel, Uni, Eliav, Ma’aseyah, and Benayah \add played harps in a certain musical style\add*.
\v 21 Matityah, Elifelehu, Mikneyah, Oved-Edom, Yeiel, and Azazyah \add played lyres in a different musical style\add*.
\p
\v 22 Kenanyah, the leader of the musical Levites, \add directed the singing because he was very capable\add*.
\v 23 Berekyah and Elkanah \add were two of\add* \add the men who guarded\add* the \add sacred\add* box.
\v 24 The priests Shevanyah, Yoshafat, Netanel, Amasay, Zekaryah, Benayah, and Eliezer were the ones who blew trumpets in front of God’s \add sacred\add* box. Oved-Edom and Yehiyah \add also\add* guarded the \add sacred\add* box.
\p
\v 25 \add So\add* David, the Israeli elders, and the \add top army commanders\add* went to \add get\add* the box \add containing\add* Yahweh’s agreement from Oved-Edom’s house with \add much\add* \add celebration\add*.
\v 26 \add Indeed,\add* God helped the Levites carrying the box \add containing\add* Yahweh’s agreement, and they slaughtered seven bulls and seven rams.
\v 27 David was wearing a fine-linen robe, \add and so were\add* all the Levites carrying the box, and the singers, and \add the choir director\add* Kenanyah. \add In addition,\add* David \add wore\add* a linen chestpiece.
\v 28 \add So\add* all of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* brought the box \add containing\add* Yahweh’s agreement up \add the hill\add* with happy shouting, and with a sound of a \add ram’s\add* horn, and with trumpets and cymbals, \add along with the sound of\add* harps and lyres.
\p
\v 29 \add As\add* the box \add containing\add* Yahweh’s agreement was being brought inside the city of David, Mikal (Sha’ul’s \add (Saul)\add* daughter) looked out the window and saw \add her husband,\add* King David, dancing and celebrating, and she \add secretly\add* despised him.
\c 16
\s1 The sacred box is placed in the tent
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then they brought in God’s box and put it in the middle of the tent that David had \add erected\add* for it, and they offered burnt offerings and peace offerings \add in front\add* of God.
\v 2 \add When\add* David finished \add presenting\add* the burnt offering and the peace offerings, he blessed the people in Yahweh’s name.
\v 3 Then he distributed a loaf of bread, a date cake, and a raisin cake to each man and woman in Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*.
\p
\v 4 Then \add David\add* appointed \add some Levites to minister in front\add* of Yahweh’s box and to commemorate and to thank and to praise Yisrael’s god Yahweh.
\v 5 Asaf was their leader, with Zekaryah \add (Zechariah)\add* as his assistant. The other Levites who helped were Yeiel, Shemiramot, Yehiel, Matityah, Eliav, Benayah, Oved-Edom, and Yeiel. \add They played\add* harps and lyres, and Asaf played cymbals.
\v 6 Benayah and Yahaziel the priests regularly \add blew\add* trumpets \add in front of\add* the box \add containing\add* God’s agreement.
\s1 A praise song
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (Psa. 105:1-15; 96:1-3; 106:1,47-48)
\p
\v 7 On that day David first \add gave\add* Asaf and his helpers this song to praise Yahweh:
\q1
\v 8 Give thanks to Yahweh \add and\add* \add use his name when calling out\add* \add for help\add*.
\q1 →\add Tell all the other nations about what he’s done\add* \add for you Israelis\add*.
\q1
\v 9 Sing to him \add and\add* \add make music\add* to him.
\q1 →Tell \add everyone\add* \add about all the wonderful things that he’s done\add*.
\q1
\v 10 \add Boast about his untarnished reputation\add*.
\q1 →\add Those who follow\add* Yahweh should celebrate.
\q1
\v 11 \add Ask Yahweh to help you and give you\add* his strength.
\q1 \add Constantly ask for his direction\add*.
\q1
\v 12 Remember \add the wonderful things\add* that he’s done—
\q1 his miracles and \add how he punished those who deserved it\add*.
\q1
\v 13 You descendants of his servant Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*,
\q1 you descendants of Ya’akov \add (Jacob)\add*, the \add one\add* who he chose,
\q1
\v 14 he is our god, Yahweh.
\q1 \add He judges the entire world\add*.
\q1
\v 15 Remember his agreement forever—
\q1 the \add instructions that he gave\add* to a thousand generations,
\q1
\v 16 \add the agreement\add* that he \add made\add* with Avraham,\x + \xo 16:16: \xt Gen 12:7.\x*
\q1 and his promise to Yitshak \add (Isaac)\add*.\x + \xo 16:16: \xt Gen 26:3.\x*
\q1
\v 17 He established it as a law for Ya’akov \add (Jacob)\add*\x + \xo 16:17-18: \xt Gen 28:13.\x*—
\q1 as an \add eternal\add* agreement for Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*,
\q1
\v 18 saying, “I’ll give the Kena’an \add (Canaan)\add* \add region\add* to you—
\q1 \add your portion that will then be\add* your inheritance.”
\b
\q1
\v 19 When \add your ancestors were still a small group\add*,
\q1 insignificant, and \add strangers in the land\add*,
\q1
\v 20 and they \add wandered\add* back and forth from nation to nation,
\q1 and from one kingdom to another,
\q2
\v 21 \add God\add* didn’t permit anyone to oppress them,
\q1 and he rebuked kings on their behalf:\x + \xo 16:21-22: \xt Gen 20:3-7.\x*
\q1
\v 22 “Don’t \add attack\add* my \add chosen people\add*,
\q1 and don’t harm my prophets.”
\b
\q1
\v 23 Sing to Yahweh \add everybody\add*.
\q1 →\add Every day tell others that he’s saved us\add*.
\q1
\v 24 \add Tell the other nations about his power\add*.
\q1 \add Tell all the people groups about the wonderful things he’s done\add*,
\q1
\v 25 because Yahweh is great and deserves a lot of praise,
\q1 and he should be revered more than any other god.
\q1
\v 26 All those gods of the \add other\add* people groups are worthless,
\q1 ^\add but it was Yahweh who created\add* the heavens.
\q1
\v 27 \add His presence emanates splendour and grandeur\add*,
\q1 \add and\add* strength and happiness \add radiate\add* from his place.
\b
\q1
\v 28 \add You people in nations all over the world, praise\add* Yahweh—
\q1 \add praise him for his splendour and his\add* strength.
\q1
\v 29 \add Praise Yahweh for his great reputation\add*.
\q1 Bring an offering and come \add into his presence\add*.
\q1 Worship Yahweh in \add his\add* \add splendour and purity\add*.
\q1
\v 30 \add Let the entire planet tremble if he comes\add*.
\q1 The world is \add firmly\add* established—it can’t be shaken \add around\add*.
\q1
\v 31 Let the heavens be glad, and let the earth shout in celebration,
\q1 and let \add people in all\add* the nations say, “Yahweh \add is king\add*.”
\q1
\v 32 Let the oceans roar \add along with all the creatures in them\add*.
\q1 Let the countryside celebrate \add along with all the creatures that live there\add*.
\q1
\v 33 Then the trees in the \add forests\add* should shout happily \add when Yahweh comes\add*,
\q1 because he is coming to judge the \add world\add*.
\b
\q1
\v 34 Give thanks to Yahweh, because he’s good,
\q1 because his loyal commitment \add lasts\add* forever.\x + \xo 16:34: \xt 2Ch 5:13; 7:3; Ezr 3:11; Psa 100:5; 106:1; 107:1; 118:1; 136:1; Jer 33:11.\x*
\q1
\v 35 Then say, “Rescue us, God \add who’s our saviour\add*,
\q1 and bring us together and save us from \add attacks\add* by other nations,
\q1 so that we’ll thank you for your \add untarnished reputation\add*,
\q1 and boast about \add your praiseworthy actions\add*.”
\q1
\v 36 Blessed be Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* god Yahweh,
\q1 \add in eternity past and eternity to come\add*.
\q1 And all the people said, “Amen” and praised Yahweh.
\s1 Worshipping in Yerushalem
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 37 Then \add David\add* went away from where Yahweh’s box had been placed. Asaf and his brothers were left to minister \add in front\add* of the box continually—\add in the appropriate way each day depending on the occasion\add*—
\v 38 \add along with\add* Oved-Edom and sixty-eight relatives. Oved-Edom (Yedutun’s son) and Hosah were gatekeepers.
\p
\v 39 Also, Tsadok the priest and his relatives the priests \add were at the front\add* of Yahweh’s tabernacle at the high place, which was in Giveon,
\v 40 to continually present burnt offerings to Yahweh on the altar for burnt offerings, morning and evening, \add following all the instructions Yahweh had given Yisrael\add* \add (Israel)\add* \add , which had been written down\add*.
\v 41 With them were Heyman and Yedutun and the rest of those chosen, who were \add designated by name\add* to thank Yahweh because his loyal commitment \add lasts\add* forever.
\v 42 Heyman and Yedutun had brought trumpets and cymbals to accompany the songs for God, and Yedutun’s sons at the gate \add joined in also\add*.
\p
\v 43 Then all the people left and went home, and David \add also\add* returned \add home\add* to bless his household.\x + \xo 16:43: \xt 2Sam 6:19-20.\x*
\c 17
\s1 Natan’s message for David
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 7:1-17)
\p
\v 1 \add After a while, when\add* David was living in his house, \add he\add* said to the prophet Natan \add (Nathan)\add*, “Look, \add here\add* I am living in a \add palace\add* \add made\add* of cedar, \add but\add* the box \add containing\add* Yahweh’s agreement is \add kept\add* \add in a tent\add*.”
\v 2 “\add Go and do what you’re planning\add*,” Natan \add replied\add*, “because God is with you.”
\p
\v 3 \add But that night God spoke\add* to Natan,
\v 4 “Go and tell my servant David that Yahweh says this: You yourself \add won’t be the one to\add* build a residence for me to live in,
\v 5 because I haven’t lived in a building from the day that I brought Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* \add out of Mitsrayim\add* \add (Egypt)\add* until today—I’ve \add moved from place to place and tent to tent\add*.
\v 6 \add As I travelled with all the Israelis\add*, \add did I ever complain to any of Yisrael’s leaders\add* who I’d commanded to shepherd my people, \add and ask them\add*, ‘Why haven’t you \add people\add* built a house out of cedar for me?’ ”
\p
\v 7 “\add So now you should tell\add* my servant David that Commander-in-chief Yahweh says this: I myself took you from the pasture, from behind the sheep, to be a leader over my people, Yisrael.
\v 8 I’ve always been with you wherever you’ve gone, and I’ve \add defeated all your enemies who were attacking you\add*, and I’ll \add make you famous like some of the greatest people in the world\add*.
\v 9 I’ll choose a place for my people, Yisrael, and I’ll plant them, and they’ll settle \add in that place\add* and \add not need to be fearful again\add*. \add Violent groups won’t continue to trouble them like they did in the past\add*,
\v 10 \add ever since I first put heroes and leaders\add* over my people, Yisrael. Then I’ll humble all your enemies. \add In addition\add*, I declare to you that Yahweh will build \add a dynasty\add* for you.
\v 11 \add When your time on earth is up, and it’s time to join your deceased ancestors\add*, then I’ll raise up one of your sons and establish his kingdom.
\v 12 He’ll \add be the one to\add* build a temple for me and establish a \add dynasty that will last\add* forever.
\v 13 I myself will be a father to him, and he’ll be a son to me,\x + \xo 17:13: \xt 2Cor 6:18; Heb 1:5.\x* and I won’t \add cease my loyal commitment to him like I ended it with your predecessor\add*.
\v 14 I’ll \add confirm his rule over my temple and my kingdom forever, and his ruling dynasty will last\add* forever.”
\p
\v 15 So Natan \add (Nathan)\add* \add passed all of that on\add* to David.
\s1 David praises Yahweh
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 7:18-29)
\p
\v 16 Then king David went in and sat \add in front\add* of Yahweh, and \add asked\add*, “Who am I, Yahweh God, and what is my \add family\add*, that you’ve brought me \add to this point\add*?
\v 17 This was such a small thing in your eyes, God, and you’ve spoken about \add the future of your servant’s family\add*, and you’ve \add blessed me more than I deserve\add*, Yahweh God.
\v 18 What more can \add I\add*, David, say to you \add about how\add* you’ve honoured your servant? You \add certainly know me well\add*.
\v 19 Yahweh, you’ve done all these \add great things\add* for the sake of your servant, and \add done just what you wanted in order to reveal your greatness\add*.
\v 20 Yahweh, there’s no one else like you, and no god except you, \add as we’ve all heard for ourselves\add*.
\v 21 \add There’s no other country\add* like your people, Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*—the only nation in the world where \add you\add*, God, rescued the people in order to make them your own. That made you famous, and also feared, as you drove the people groups out ahead of your people who you ransomed out of Mitsrayim \add (Egypt)\add*.
\v 22 You took your people, Yisrael, to be your people forever, and you, Yahweh, became our god.
\p
\v 23 “And now, Yahweh, may \add what you said\add* about your servant and his \add family\add* be confirmed forever, and \add come to pass\add* just like you’ve said.
\v 24 May your \add reputation\add* be established and increase forever, as \add people\add* say, ‘Commander-in-chief Yahweh, Yisrael’s god, is god over Yisrael.’ And your servant David’s \add family\add* has been established \add as you’ve watched\add*,
\v 25 because you, God, \add have revealed to your servant that you’ll give me a dynasty\add*. That’s why I’m \add brave enough\add* to pray to you.
\v 26 \add Yes,\add* Yahweh, you are God and you’ve promised these good things to \add me,\add* your servant.
\v 27 And you’ve now decided to bless your servant’s \add family\add* to be \add in front of you\add* forever, because you, Yahweh, have blessed \add us\add*, and so \add we’ll\add* be blessed forever.
\c 18
\s1 David’s victories
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 8:1-18)
\p
\v 1 \add Sometime afterwards\add*, David \add attacked and defeated\add* the Pelishtim, \add subduing\add* them, and he \add captured the Pelishti city of Gat and its surrounding villages\add*.
\v 2 Then he \add attacked and defeated\add* Moav \add (Moab)\add*, and the Moavim became servants to David—\add taking him\add* \add regular\add* tribute.
\p
\v 3 Next David \add attacked and defeated\add* King Hadadezer of Tsovah-Hamat, when \add that king had tried enforce his control\add* at the Perat \add (Euphrates)\add* \add River\add*.
\v 4 David captured 1,000 chariots, 7,000 horsemen, and 20,000 foot-soldiers from him. And David hamstrung all but one hundred of the chariot \add horses\add*.
\p
\v 5 When the Arameans \add (or, Aramim\add* \add (Syrians)\add* \add )\add* came from Damesek \add (Damascus)\add* to help King Hadadezer, David killed 22,000 of them.
\v 6 Then David \add established forces\add* in the Aramean \add town\add* of Damesek, and the Arameans became servants to David, \add taking him\add* \add regular\add* tribute. \add So\add* Yahweh \add helped\add* David \add wherever\add* he went.
\v 7 David took the gold shields that were used by Hadadezer’s servants and brought them to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*.
\v 8 \add He\add* also took a lot of bronze from Hadadezer’s cities of Tivhat and Kun. (\add Later,\add* Shelomoh \add (Solomon)\add* used it to make the \add huge bronze basin called ‘the sea’, along with its\add* pillars and other bronze items.)\x + \xo 18:8: \xt 1Ki 7:40-47; 2Ch 4:11-18.\x*
\p
\v 9 When King Toeu from Hamat heard that David \add had defeated\add* King Hadadezer’s entire army from Tsovah,
\v 10 he sent his son Hadoram to King David carrying various items of gold, silver, and bronze. \add Hadoram went\add* to ask \add David\add* for peace and to bless him because he’d fought against Hadadezer and struck him down, because Toeu had \add also\add* \add been at war\add* with Hadadezer.
\v 11 King David set that apart as holy to Yahweh, along with the gold and silver that he’d brought back from \add other\add* nations: from Edom, from Moav \add (Moab)\add*, from the \add Amonim\add*, from the Pelishtim, and from \add the Amalekim\add*.
\p
\v 12 Avishay (whose \add mother\add* was Tseruyah) defeated Edom in the Salt Valley, \add killing\add* eighteen thousand \add of them\add*.\x + \xo 18:12: \xt Psa 60 header.\x*
\v 13 Then \add David\add* stationed garrisons in Edom, and all of Edom became his servants. And Yahweh saved David wherever he went.
\p
\v 14 \add So\add* David ruled over all of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* and administered justice and \add did what was right\add* for all his people.
\v 15 Tseruyah’s son Yoav \add (Joab)\add* led the army, and Ahilud’s son Yehoshafat \add (Jehoshaphat)\add* was the record keeper.
\v 16 Ahituv’s son Tsadok and Evyatar’s son Ahimelek were priests, and Shavsha was the secretary.
\v 17 Yehoyada’s son Benayah \add supervised\add* the Keretim and Peletim \add who were David’s bodyguards\add*, and David’s sons \add were his leading officials\add*.
\c 19
\s1 The defeat of the Amonim and Arameans
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 10:1-19)
\p
\v 1 \add Some time later\add*, the Amoni King Nahash died and his son \add Hanun\add* \add became their king\add*,
\v 2 and David said, “I’ll show loyal commitment to Nahash’s son Hanun, because his father \add was good to me\add*.” \add So\add* David sent messengers to comfort him concerning his father’s \add death\add*. \add However,\add* when David’s servants \add entered Amoni territory\add* to comfort Hanun,
\v 3 the Amoni leaders \add asked their new king\add*, “\add Do you think that\add* David’s \add really\add* sending comforters to honour your father? \add Wouldn’t it be more likely that he sent his servants to explore and spy out the land in order to overthrow you\add*?”
\v 4 \add So\add* \add King\add* Hanun took David’s servants and \add had their beards\add* shaved off, plus he cut \add off the bottom half of their robes to expose their\add* buttocks, and then he sent them \add home\add*.
\v 5 \add Some people quickly informed David what had happened\add*, so he sent \add messengers\add* to meet the men (because they were very much humiliated) and to tell them, “Stay \add there\add* in Yeriho \add (Jericho)\add* until your \add beards\add* grow \add out\add*, then return \add here\add*.”
\p
\v 6 \add By then\add*, the Amonim \add realised that they’d greatly insulted\add* \add King\add* David, \add so\add* \add King\add* Hanun and the Amonim sent thirty tonnes of silver to hire chariots and horsemen from Aram-Naharayim, Aram-Ma’akah, and Tsovah.
\v 7 They hired 32,000 chariots, \add as well as\add* the king of Ma’akah and his army. They \add all\add* came and \add set up their camps near\add* Meydeva, \add plus they were joined by\add* the Amonim \add themselves\add* coming out of their cities ready for battle.
\p
\v 8 \add When\add* David heard \add about that\add*, he sent Yoav \add (Joab)\add* \add with the entire\add* army.
\v 9 Then the Amonim marched out and deployed ready for battle at the entrance to \add their\add* city, while the kings who’d come were over in the countryside \add with their men\add*.
\p
\v 10 Yoav \add quickly\add* realised that \add he’d have to fight on two fronts, so\add* he chose Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* best warriors and sent them out to face the Arameans \add (Syrians)\add*,
\v 11 while he sent the remaining men (under the \add control\add* of his \add older brother\add* Avishay) out against the Amonim.
\v 12 \add Yoav\add* told them, “If the Arameans are too strong for me, then you \add can come to rescue me\add*. \add But\add* if the Amonim \add start to gain on you\add*, then I’ll \add come to\add* \add rescue\add* you.
\v 13 We must be strong and fight hard to defend our people and our cities \add that belong to\add* our god. Yahweh will do \add what he considers to be\add* good.”
\p
\v 14 \add So\add* Yoav \add advanced his troops to attack the Arameans, but the Arameans fled away from them\add*.
\v 15 \add When\add* the Amonim saw that the Arameans had fled, \add they also fled from Avishay and withdrew into\add* the city, \add so\add* Yoav went back to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*.
\p
\v 16 \add When the Arameans\add* saw that they were defeated \add by\add* Yisrael, they sent messengers to bring \add more Arameans from across the Perat\add* \add (Euphrates)\add* \add river, led by\add* Shofak, the commander of Hadadezer’s army.
\v 17 \add When\add* that was reported to David, he gathered all of Yisrael and crossed the Yarden \add (Jordan)\add*. \add They\add* positioned themselves against the Arameans, who then fought against them.
\v 18 \add But the Arameans fled away from the Israelis\add*, and David killed seven thousand chariot \add drivers\add* and forty thousand men on foot, as well as killing Shofak, \add their\add* army commander.
\v 19 When Hadadezer’s servants saw that \add Yisrael had defeated them\add*, they made peace with David and \add became his subjects, so\add* Aram \add (Syria)\add* wasn’t willing to help the Amonim \add militarily\add* any more.
\c 20
\s1 David captures Rabah
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 12:26-31)
\p
\v 1 \add Then, in the spring, when kings often march out to war\add*, Yoav \add (Joab)\add* led the army out for war, and he devastated the Amonim’ land. Then he reached \add the capital,\add* Rabah, and he besieged it while David stayed in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*.\x + \xo 20:1: \xt 2Sam 11:1.\x* Yoav \add defeated\add* Rabah and \add demolished the city\add*.
\v 2 Then David took their king’s crown off his head—it was gold with a valuable gemstone set in it, and \add weighed thirty kilograms\add*. He put \add the crown\add* on \add his\add* own head, plus he took a very large amount of plunder out of the city.
\v 3 Then they took all the people out of the city and \add forced them to work\add* with saws, iron picks, and axes. David did that to the people from all the Amoni cities, then \add he\add* returned to Yerushalem with his army.
\s1 Battles against Gitim
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 21:15-22)
\p
\v 4 \add Some time later,\add* war broke out with the Pelishtim at Gezer, then Sibkay the Hushati struck down Sipay, from the descendants of the Refaim, and they were subdued.
\p
\v 5 Then there was another battle with the Pelishtim, and Yair’s son Elhanan \add killed\add* Lahmi the brother of Galyat \add (Goliath)\add*\x + \xo 20:5: \xt 1Sam 17:4-7.\x* the Giti \add (that is, from Gat)\add*, even though his spear shaft was \add as thick\add* as the beam of a weaver’s \add loom\add*.
\v 6 There was also war at Gat, where there was a \add very tall man with six fingers on each hand and six toes on each foot, who was also a descendant\add* of Rafa.
\v 7 \add When\add* he insulted Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*, Shimea’s son Yonatan \add (Jonathan)\add* killed him. (Shimea was one of David’s \add older\add* \add brothers\add*.)
\p
\v 8 Those were the descendants of Rafa from Gat who were killed by David and his men.
\c 21
\s1 David orders a census
\s4 Full kingdom
\r (2 Sam. 24:1-25)
\s2 Yoav doesn’t want to do the census
\p
\v 1 Then a trouble-maker worked against Yisrael \add (Israel)\add* \add by\add* encouraging David \add to take a census of\add* Yisrael,
\v 2 \add so\add* David \add ordered\add* Yoav \add (Joab)\add* and \add the other officials\add*, “Go \add and\add* count Yisrael from Be’er-Sheva \add (Beersheba)\add* \add in the south to all the way\add* as far \add north\add* as Dan, \add then come and tell me, so that I’ll know how many warriors I have\add*.”
\p
\v 3 “My master, the king, may Yahweh \add multiply his people one hundred times over\add*. Aren’t they all \add your\add* servants?” Yoav \add diplomatically\add* \add queried\add*, “\add So\add* why does my master \add want to do\add* this? Why should \add he bring judgement on\add* Yisrael?”
\v 4 \add But the king insisted, so Yoav was forced to go and gather statistics on the entire country\add*. Then he returned to Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*
\v 5 and \add reported the numbers\add* to David. Yisrael had 1.1 million sword-wielding men, and Yehudah \add (Judah)\add* had 470 thousand.
\v 6 \add However,\add* \add Yoav\add* had \add intentionally\add* not counted \add the tribes of\add* Levi and Benyamin, because \add he\add* was horrified by the king’s command.
\s2 Punishment for the census
\p
\v 7 God \add was displeased by what David had ordered, and he punished\add* Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*,
\v 8 and David said to God, “I’ve \add disobeyed you badly by doing that\add*. Please \add forgive your servant for his disobedience\add*, because I’ve \add been very stupid\add*.”
\p
\v 9 Then Yahweh told David’s prophet Gad,
\v 10 “Go and tell David that Yahweh says this: \add I’ll give you three options—choose one of them and that’s what I’ll\add* do to you.”
\p
\v 11 \add So\add* Gad went to David and told him, “Yahweh says that you must choose \add one of these punishments\add*:
\v 12 either three years of famine, or three months of being overcome by your enemies’ \add armies\add*, or three days of Yahweh’s ‘sword’ of pestilence in the country and Yahweh’s messenger \add bringing destruction\add* across all of Yisrael’s territory. \add So now, tell me what answer I should take\add* back to the one who sent me.”
\p
\v 13 “\add Oh,\add* that’s very distressing,” David \add replied\add* to Gad, “but please don’t let me fall into \add my enemies’\add* hands. Yahweh \add is very merciful, so please let him be the one to punish me directly\add*.”
\p
\v 14 \add So\add* Yahweh \add sent a disease\add* throughout Yisrael, and seventy thousand Israeli men \add died\add*.
\v 15 Then God sent a messenger to destroy Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*, and \add while the disease was still spreading,\add* Yahweh \add looked down\add* and relented about the tragedy, and he told the destroying messenger, “Enough! Now \add stop what you’re doing\add*.” Now Yahweh’s messenger \add happened to be\add* standing at the threshing floor belonging to Ornan the Yevusi.
\p
\v 16 \add As\add* David \add looked up\add*, he saw Yahweh’s messenger standing between the earth and the heavens, holding his sword stretched out over Yerushalem. Then David and the elders, who were \add all\add* dressed in sackcloth, fell \add to their knees and bowed their faces to the ground\add*.
\v 17 Then David \add asked\add* God, “Wasn’t it me myself who said to count the people? It’s me who \add disobeyed and clearly did what was wrong\add*, but these sheep, what have they done? Yahweh my god, please \add direct your punishment\add* against me and \add my extended family\add*, but \add don’t continue this\add* \add deadly disease\add* on your people.”
\p
\v 18 Then Yahweh’s messenger told Gad to tell David that \add he\add* should \add build\add* an altar to Yahweh at Ornan the Yevusi’s threshing floor.
\v 19 \add So\add* David went up \add to that place when he heard\add* \add Gad’s message that was delivered\add* in Yahweh’s name.
\v 20 \add Meanwhile,\add* Ornan was threshing wheat, and \add he\add* turned and saw \add Yahweh’s\add* messenger, and he and his four sons who were with him \add quickly\add* hid \add behind something\add*.
\v 21 \add When David arrived there,\add* Ornan saw \add him\add* and left the threshing floor and \add knelt\add* in front of David, bowing \add his\add* face to the ground.
\v 22 Then David \add asked\add* Ornan, “Give me this place with its threshing floor, and I’ll build an altar to Yahweh on it. \add Sell it to me\add* at the full price, so the \add deadly disease affecting\add* the people will be stopped.”
\p
\v 23 “My master the king, take \add anything you want\add*,” Ornan \add replied\add*. “Listen, I’ll donate the cattle for the burnt offerings, and the threshing sledges for firewood, and the wheat for the offering—all \add at no charge\add*.”
\p
\v 24 “No, I’ll most definitely pay the full price,” King David \add responded\add*. “I couldn’t offer a burnt offering to Yahweh \add if it hadn’t cost me anything\add*.”
\v 25 \add So\add* David paid Ornan six hundred gold pieces for \add his\add* place,
\v 26 and \add he\add* built an altar to Yahweh there and offered burnt offerings and peace offerings on it. He called to Yahweh, and \add Yahweh\add* answered him with fire from the heavens on that altar with the burnt offering.
\p
\v 27 Then Yahweh spoke to \add his\add* messenger, who then returned his sword to his sheath.
\s1 The location for the future temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 28 When David saw that Yahweh had answered him at Ornan the Yevusi’s threshing floor, he sacrificed there.
\v 29 \add At that time\add*, Yahweh’s \add sacred tent\add* that Mosheh \add (Moses)\add* had made in the wilderness, and the altar for burnt sacrifices, were \add on a hill used for worship near\add* Giveon \add city\add*.
\v 30 David wasn’t able to go there to \add ask God’s direction\add* because he was terrified of Yahweh’s messenger’s sword.
\c 22
\p
\v 1 Then David said, “This is \add now\add* the residence of Yahweh God, and this is the altar for Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* burnt \add offering\add*.”
\s1 The preparation of standing up of temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 2 Then David said to gather the foreigners who \add lived\add* in Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*, and he assigned \add them the job of\add* hewing out cut stones to build the residence for God.
\v 3 \add He\add* prepared a large quantity of iron for the door \add hinges and fittings\add*, so much brass that it couldn’t be weighed,
\v 4 and innumerable cedar logs that the Tsidonim \add (Sidonians)\add* and the Tsorim \add (Tyrians)\add* brought in for David.
\v 5 David said, “My son Shelomoh \add (Solomon)\add* is young and not capable, and the house to be built for Yahweh must be \add impressive for the sake of his reputation\add* and honour everywhere, \add so\add* I’ll prepare for it now.” \add So\add* David did a lot of \add preparation\add* before \add he died\add*.
\p
\v 6 Then \add David\add* called for his son Shelomoh, and ordered him to build a residence for Yisrael’s god Yahweh.
\v 7 David told \add him\add*, “My son, I wanted to build a temple to honour my god Yahweh,\x + \xo 22:7-10: \xt 2Sam 7:1-16; 1Ch 17:1-14.\x*
\v 8 \add but\add* Yahweh \add gave me this message\add*, ‘You’ve fought a lot of battles and shed a large amount of blood, \add so\add* you won’t be the one who’ll build a residence \add to honour me\add*—you’ve shed too much blood on the ground \add in my sight\add*.’
\v 9 \add He told me,\add* ‘Listen, you’ll have a son who’ll be a man of peace. I’ll give him relief from all his enemies from the surrounding regions. That’s why he’ll be called ‘Shelomoh’ \add (which sounds like ‘Shalom’—the Hebrew word for peace)\add*, and I’ll give peace and quietness to Yisrael during his lifetime.
\v 10 He’ll be the one to build a residence for me, and he himself will become my son, and I’ll \add become\add* his father, and I’ll establish his kingdom’s \add authority\add* over Yisrael forever.’
\p
\v 11 “Now, my son, may Yahweh be with you, and may you be successful and build a residence for Yahweh your god, just as he said you would.
\v 12 \add I also ask\add* Yahweh to give you insight and understanding, and give you charge over Yisrael so that you can keep your god Yahweh’s regulations.
\v 13 Then you’ll succeed, if you’re careful to follow the instructions and the directions that Yahweh commanded Mosheh \add (Moses)\add* concerning Yisrael. Be strong and courageous. Don’t be afraid or discouraged.\x + \xo 22:13: \xt Josh 1:6-9.\x*
\p
\v 14 Now, see, \add despite many battles\add*, I’ve prepared the following for Yahweh’s residence: three thousand tonnes of gold and thirty thousand tonnes of silver, along with too much bronze and iron to weigh, and timber and \add cut\add* stones. Plus you’ll be able to add more.
\v 15 \add You’ll have plenty of skilled workers, including those who work with stone and those who work with wood\add*.
\v 16 \add Then there’s so many skilled with gold and silver, and bronze and iron. So get organised and get started\add*, and may Yahweh be with you.
\p
\v 17 Then David ordered all the Israeli officials to assist his son Shelomoh \add (Solomon)\add*, \add saying\add*,
\v 18 “Isn’t Yahweh your god with you \add officials\add*, and hasn’t he given you peace \add on every side\add*? He’s \add allowed me to take control of this region, and this area has been subdued with the help\add* of Yahweh and his people.
\v 19 Now, \add dedicate your minds and strength to requesting advice from your god Yahweh, then get organised and build Yahweh God’s sanctuary so you can bring the box containing Yahweh’s agreement to the residence to be built to honour Yahweh’s name, and bring God’s sacred items\add*.”
\c 23
\p
\v 1 \add By then David was old and coming to the end of his life\add*, and he made his son Shelomoh king of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*.\x + \xo 23:1: \xt 1Ki 1:1-40.\x*
\s1 The Levite families and their assignments
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 2 Then \add David\add* gathered all the Israeli officials and the priests and Levites.
\v 3 Counting the Levite males that were thirty years old and older, they found there were thirty-eight thousand men.
\v 4 \add David assigned\add* twenty-four thousand of them to supervise the work at Yahweh’s residence, six thousand to be officials and judges,
\v 5 four thousand to be gatekeepers, and four thousand to praise Yahweh using the musical instruments that he’d provided.
\p
\v 6 \add So\add* David divided \add the Levites\add* into divisions according to \add their ancestry through\add* Levi’s sons Gereshon, Kohat, and Merari.
\s1 The Gershonim
\p
\v 7 The Gershonim \add included\add* Laedan and Shimei.
\v 8 Laedan’s three sons were: Yehiel (the oldest), Zetam, and Yoel \add (Joel)\add*.
\v 9 Shimei’s\f + \fr 23:9 \ft Possibly the wrong name got into the Hebrew here, because it disagrees with v10.\f* three sons were: Shelomot, Haziel, and Haran. (They were leaders of Laedan’s clans.)
\v 10 Shimei’s four sons were: Yahat, Zina,\f + \fr 23:10 \ft Some ancient translations have Zizah here, which would seem to fit better with v11.\f* Ye’ush, and Beriah.
\v 11 (Yahat was the head, and Zizah was second. Ye’ush and Beriah were \add grouped together as one family unit because they didn’t have many descendants\add*.)
\s2 The Kohatites
\p
\v 12 Kohat’s four sons were: Amram, Yitshar, Hevron, and Uziel.
\v 13 Amram’s sons were Aharon \add (Aaron)\add* and Mosheh \add (Moses)\add*. Aharon \add and his future male descendants were permanently appointed\add* to consecrate the most sacred things, to burn incense, to minister to Yahweh, and to bless his name.\x + \xo 23:13: \xt Exo 28:1.\x*
\v 14 Mosheh \add was known as\add* \add a man who served\add* God, and his descendants were included with the \add other\add* Levites.
\v 15 Mosheh’s sons were Gereshom and Eliezer.
\v 16 Gereshom’s eldest \add son\add* was Shevuel.
\v 17 Eliezer’s only son was Rehavyah, but Rehavyah \add went on to\add* have many sons.
\p
\v 18 Yitshar’s eldest \add son\add* was Shelomot.
\v 19 Hevron’s sons \add from oldest to youngest\add* were: Yeriyah, Amaryah, Yahaziel, and Yekameam.
\v 20 Uziel’s sons were Mikah and Yishiyah.
\s2 The Merarim
\p
\v 21 Merari’s sons were Mahli and Mushi, \add and\add* Mahli’s sons were Eleazar and Kish.
\v 22 Eleazar died without having any sons, \add so\add* his brother Kish’s sons married his daughters \add (to continue the family name)\add*.
\v 23 Mushi had three sons: Mahli, Eder, and Yerimot.
\p
\v 24 Those were Levi’s descendants \add by their clans and their appointments, who did the work of serving in Yahweh’s residence once they reached twenty years old\add*,
\v 25 because David had said, “Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* god Yahweh has given peace to his people, and he’ll \add now\add* live forever in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*,
\v 26 \add so\add* the Levites won’t need to carry the \add sacred tent\add* anymore, along with all its equipment.”\x + \xo 23:26: \xt Deu 10:8.\x*
\v 27 \add So one of David’s final instructions had been to count the Levite men who were twenty years old and over\add*,
\v 28 because their work was to \add assist\add* Aharon’s \add (Aaron)\add* descendants in their work in Yahweh’s residence.\x + \xo 23:28-32: \xt Num 3:5-9.\x* They \add were in charge\add* of the courtyards and siderooms, \add in charge of the purification ceremonies\add*, and did other work at God’s residence.
\v 29 \add They were also in charge of\add* the \add sacred\add* bread, as well as the fine flour for the offering, the wafers of bread without raising agent, the baking pans, and \add all the measuring and mixing of ingredients\add*.
\p
\v 30 Every morning and every evening \add the Levites\add* stood and praised Yahweh.
\v 31 They would also do that on the rest days, and at the new moon celebrations and during the other religious festivals. \add There were regulations about how many of them must always be on duty serving\add* Yahweh.
\v 32 They were \add also\add* \add in charge of\add* the sacred tent and the holy place, and assisting \add the priests\add* (their relatives, Aharon’s descendants) in their service at Yahweh’s residence.
\c 24
\s1 The Levites’ duties
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Aharon’s \add (Aaron)\add* sons were: Nadav, Avihu, Eleazar, and Itamar,
\v 2 \add but\add* Nadav and Avihu died \add before their father\add* and \add without having any children,\x + \xo 24:2: \xt Lev 10:1-2.\x* so\add* \add only\add* Eleazar and Itamar became priests.
\v 3 David \add gave Eleazar’s descendant Tsadok, and Itamar’s descendant Ahimelek two different sets of duties to perform\add*.
\v 4 \add There were more leaders among Eleazar’s descendants than there were among Itamar’s descendants, so they appointed sixteen leaders from Eleazar’s descendants and eight from Itamar’s descendants\add*.
\v 5 They divided up \add the duties by a process of choosing randomly, so some of the leaders from each of the two groups served the facility and others served God directly\add*. DOUBLE-CHECK, along with verse 3 as well.
\v 6 Netanel’s son Shemayah was the Levite scribe who recorded all this in front of the king and the leaders, Tsadok the priest and Evyatar’s son Ahimelek, and the clan leaders of the priests and the Levites. (One \add family\add* from Eleazar’s group was randomly assigned a duty, and then one from Itamar’s group, and so on.)
\p
\v 7 The first two \add allocations of duties\add* went to Yehoyariv and then Yedayah.
\v 8 Next were Harim and Se’orim,
\v 9 Malkiyah and Miyamin,
\v 10 Hakots and Aviyah,
\v 11 Yeshua and Shekanyah,
\v 12 Elyashiv and Yakim,
\v 13 Hupah and Yeshev’av,
\v 14 Bilgah and Imer,
\v 15 Hezir and Hapitsets,
\v 16 Petahyah and Yehezkel,
\v 17 Yakin and Gemul,
\v 18 Delayah and, lastly, Ma’azyah.
\p
\v 19 Those \add were the priests\add* selected to \add be leaders of the groups\add* that would serve in Yahweh’s temple, following the instructions that Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* god Yahweh had given their ancestor Aharon \add (Aaron)\add*.
\s2 Other Levites
\p
\v 20 This is a list of some of Levi’s other descendants:
\li1 From Amram’s sons: Shuvael.
\li1 From Shuvael’s sons: Yehdiyah.
\li1
\v 21 From Rehavyah’s sons: Yishiyah, the leader.
\li1
\v 22 From the Yitshari: Shelomot.
\li1 From Shelomot’s sons: Yahat.
\li1
\v 23 \add Hevron’s\add*\f + \fr 24:33 \ft Missing here in the Hebrew, but see \xt 1 Chr. 23:19.\f* sons \add (in order)\add*: Yeriyah, Amaryah, Yahaziel, Yekameam
\li1
\v 24 From Uziel’s sons: Mikah.
\li1 From Mikah’s sons: Shamir.
\li1
\v 25 Mikah’s younger brother: Yishiyah.
\li1 From Yishiyah’s sons: Zekaryah \add (Zechariah)\add*.
\li1
\v 26 Merari’s sons: Mahli and Mushi.
\li1 Ya’aziyah’s son: Beno.
\li1
\v 27 Merari’s descendants through Ya’aziyah: Beno, Shoham, Zakur, and Ivri.
\li1
\v 28 From Mahli: Eleazar who had no sons.
\li1
\v 29 Kish’s \add son\add*: Yerahme’el.
\li1
\v 30 Mushi’s sons: Mahli, Eder, and Yerimot.
\p Those were the Levites listed by their ancestors’ families.
\v 31 They, too, used \add a process of choosing randomly\add*, just like Aharon’s \add (Aaron)\add* descendants (their relatives), \add to determine their specific duties\add* irrespective of where they came in their families. This was done in front of King David, Tsadok and Ahimelek, the clan leaders, and the \add other\add* priests and Levites.
\c 25
\s1 The temple singers
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then David and the army chiefs allocated the \add musical\add* service to the sons of Asaf, Heyman, and Yedutun—the ones prophesying with harps, with lyres, and with cymbals. \add This is a list of the men they chose for that work\add*:
\li1
\v 2 From Asaf’s sons: Zakur, Yosef \add (Joseph)\add*, Netanyah, and Asarelah. They were supervised by Asaf, who prophesied \add under the king’s direction\add*.
\li1
\v 3 Six from Yedutun’s family: Gedalyah, Tseri, Yeshayah \add (Isaiah)\add*, Hashavyah, and Matityah. They were supervised by Yedutun, who prophesied with a harp, and thanked and praised Yahweh.
\li1
\v 4 From Heyman’s sons: Bukiyah, Matanyah, Uziel, Shuvael, Yerimot, Hananyah, Hanani, Eliatah, Gidalti, Romamti-Ezer, Yashebekashah, Maloti, Hotir, and Mahaziot.
\v 5 They were all sons of King David’s prophet Heyman, \add who’d been promised by God that he’d strengthen him\add*, and God gave \add him\add* fourteen sons and three daughters.
\p
\v 6 All of those served as musicians in Yahweh’s temple under their \add fathers’\add* supervision—they played cymbals, harps, and lyres. \add Their fathers were supervised by\add* the king.
\v 7 There were 288 of those men and their musical relatives who played to Yahweh.
\v 8 They used \add a process of choosing randomly\add* to determine \add their\add* \add duties\add*, whether young or old, and \add irrespective of their musical abilities\add*.
\p
\v 9 The first allocation went (in order) to:
\li1 Asaf and his son Yosef \add (Joseph)\add*,
\li1 Gedalyah and his relatives and sons (a total of twelve),
\li1
\v 10 Zakur and his sons and relatives (another twelve),
\li1
\v 11 Yitsri and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 12 Netanyah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 13 Bukiyah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 14 Yesarelah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 15 Yeshayah \add (Isaiah)\add* and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 16 Matanyah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 17 Shimei and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 18 Azarel and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 19 Hashavyah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 20 Shuvael and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 21 Matityah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 22 Yerimot and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 23 Hananyah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 24 Yashebekashah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 25 Hanani and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 26 Maloti and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 27 Eliatah and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 28 Hotir and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 29 Gidalti and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 30 Mahaziot and his sons and relatives (twelve),
\li1
\v 31 Romamti-Ezer and his sons and relatives (twelve).
\c 26
\s1 The guards of doorway of temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 These were the divisions of the gatekeepers:
\li1 From the Korhim: Kore’s son Meshelemyah (Kore was one of Asaf’s sons),
\li1
\v 2 Meshelemyah’s \add seven\add* sons (in order): Zekaryah \add (Zechariah)\add*, Yedieael, Zevadyah, Yatniel,
\v 3 Eylam, Yehohanan, and Elyehoeynay.
\li1
\v 4 Oved-Edom’s\x + \xo 26:4-5: \xt 2Sam 6:11; 1Ch 13:14.\x* \add eight\add* sons (in order): Shemayah, Yehozavad, Yoah, Sakar, Netanel,
\v 5 Amiyel, Yisakar \add (Issachar)\add*, and Pe’uletay. (God had blessed him.)
\li1
\v 6 \add Oved-Edom’s\add* son Shemayah also had sons. (They were leaders in their father’s \add family\add* because they were capable warriors.)
\v 7 Shemayah’s sons were: Otni, Refael, Oved, and Elzavad. His relatives Elihu and Semakyah were also capable men.
\p
\v 8 All those descendants of Oved-Edom and their sons and relatives were capable \add people\add* and \add strong\add* workers. Altogether there were 62 of them.
\li1
\v 9 Meshelemyah had sons and relatives who were respected (eighteen in total).
\li1
\v 10 Merari’s descendant Hosah’s sons: Shimri (not \add actually\add* the firstborn, but his father appointed him as the first),
\v 11 Hilkiyah, Tevalyah, and Zekaryah \add (Zechariah)\add*. (Thirteen sons and relatives in total.)
\p
\v 12 Those men were leaders of the groups of men who guarded the temple’s gates. They worked in Yahweh’s temple like their relatives did.
\v 13 \add They used a process of choosing randomly to assign each family to a particular gate, irrespective of the family reputation\add*.
\v 14 Shelemyah’s group was allocated to the eastern gate, and his son Zekaryah (a wise adviser) was allocated to the northern gate.
\v 15 Oved-Edom’s group was allocated to the southern gate, plus his sons \add were to guard\add* the storehouses.
\v 16 Shupim’s and Hosah’s groups were allocated the western gate, along with the Shaleket gate on the uphill road, with \add side-by-side guards\add*.
\v 17 Every day there were six Levites on duty in the east, four in the north, four in the south, and two at the storehouses.
\v 18 There were four on the road by the western courtyard, and two in the courtyard.
\v 19 Those were the groups of men who were Korah’s and Merari’s descendants who guarded the \add temple\add* gates.
\s1 The guards of things/objects
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 20 The Levite Ahiyah was \add in charge of\add* the temple treasuries, as well as the storehouses for dedicated gifts.
\v 21 Laedan’s descendants (\add also\add* descendants of Gereshon) \add were leaders among their family groups. Yehiel was a descendant of this Laedan\add*.
\v 22 Yehiel’s sons were Zetam and Yoel \add (Joel)\add*, \add and they\add* \add were in charge of\add* the storehouses in Yahweh’s temple.
\p
\v 23 \add Others who did that work\add* \add were descendants of Amram, Yitshar, Hevron, and Uziel\add*.
\p
\v 24 Shuvael, a descendant of Mosheh’s \add (Moses)\add* son Gereshom, was \add another\add* leader \add in charge of\add* the treasuries,
\v 25 along with his relatives: Eliezer’s son Rehavyah, \add Rehavyah’s\add* son Yeshayah \add (Isaiah)\add*, \add Yeshayah’s\add* son Yoram \add (Joram)\add*, \add Yoram’s\add* son Zikri, and \add Zikri’s\add* son Shelomot.
\v 26 Shelomot and his relatives \add supervised\add* all the storehouses for the sacred things that were consecrated by King David, the \add clan leaders\add*, and the army division leaders and other commanders
\v 27 who’d dedicated some of the plunder \add from their battles\add* to the repair of Yahweh’s residence.
\v 28 Shelomot and his relatives were also \add in charge of\add* everything that the prophet Shmuel \add (Samuel)\add*, \add King\add* Sha’ul \add (Saul)\add* (Kish’s son), and \add David’s two army commanders\add* Avner (Ner’s son) and Yoav \add (Joab)\add* (Tseruyah’s son) had dedicated \add to Yahweh\add*.
\s1 Other Levites’ duties
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 29 From Yitshar’s descendants, Kenanyah and his sons worked outside \add the temple area\add* as officials and judges over Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*.
\p
\v 30 From Hevron’s descendants, Hashavyah and his relatives were responsible for all the work done for Yahweh and for the king in the area west of the Yarden \add (Jordan)\add* \add river\add*. There were 1,700 of them \add who were able to do their work well\add*.
\v 31 According to the records of Hevron’s descendants, Yeriyah was their leader. (In the fortieth year of David’s reign, they’d searched \add in those records\add* and discovered that there were powerful \add men\add* in Yazer in Gilead.)
\v 32 \add Yeriyah\add* had 2,700 relatives who were strong \add workers\add* and \add leaders of their families\add*. King David \add put them in charge of governing\add* the tribes of Re’uven, Gad, and the eastern half of the tribe of Menasheh \add (Manasseh)\add*, \add to ensure that all the people did what God commanded and what the king told them to do\add*.
\c 27
\s1 King David’s officials
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 This is a list of the Israeli men who served the king \add in the army\add*. Some were leaders of families, some were commanders of divisions of a thousand, some were \add commanders\add* of divisions of a hundred, and some were other officers. There were twenty-four thousand men in each group, and each group served one month each year.
\li1
\v 2 Zavdiyel’s son Yashaveam was \add in charge of\add* the group that served during the first month each year.
\v 3 \add He\add* was a descendant of Perets, and was the \add commander\add* of all the \add army officers\add* during the first month \add each year\add*.
\li1
\v 4 Doday (the Ahohi) was the commander of the group that served during the second month each year. Miklot was \add also\add* a leader in that group.
\li1
\v 5 Benayah, son of Yehoyada the high priest, was the commander of the group that served during the third month each year.
\v 6 \add He\add* was the strongest of ‘the thirty’ and was their leader. His son was Amizavad.
\li1
\v 7 The fourth \add leader\add*, for the fourth month, was Yoav’s \add (Joab)\add* brother Asahel, and then his son Zevadyah succeeded him. His division was twenty-four thousand \add men\add*.
\li1
\v 8 The fifth \add leader\add*, for the fifth month, was the commander Shamhut the Yizrah. His division was twenty-four thousand \add men\add*.
\li1
\v 9 The sixth \add leader\add*, for the sixth month, was Ikesh’s son Ira the Tekoei. His division was twenty-four thousand \add men\add*.
\li1
\v 10 The seventh \add leader\add*, for the seventh month, was Helets the Peloni (an Efrati). His division was twenty-four thousand \add men\add*.
\li1
\v 11 The eighth \add leader\add*, for the eighth month, was Sibkay the Hushati (a Zarhi). His division was twenty-four thousand \add men\add*.
\li1
\v 12 The ninth \add leader\add*, for the ninth month, was Aviezer the Antoti (a Benyamite). His division was twenty-four thousand \add men\add*.
\li1
\v 13 The tenth \add leader\add*, for the tenth month, was Maharay the Netofati (a Zarhi). His division was twenty-four thousand \add men\add*.
\li1
\v 14 The eleventh \add leader\add*, for the eleventh month, was Benayah the Pireatoni (an Efrati). His division was twenty-four thousand \add men\add*.
\li1
\v 15 The twelfth \add leader\add*, for the twelfth month, was Helday the Otnieli (a Netofati). His division was twenty-four thousand \add men\add*.
\s1 The teachers of Yisrael’s descendants
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 16 There were certain tribal leaders over each of Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* tribes:
\li1 Zikri’s son Eliezer was \add the leader of the tribe of\add* Re’uven.
\li1 Ma’akah’s son Shefatyah was \add the leader of the tribe of\add* Shim’on \add (Simeon)\add*.
\li1
\v 17 Kemuel’s son Hashavyah was \add the leader\add* of the Levites.
\li1 Tsadok was \add the leader\add* of Aharon’s \add (Aaron)\add* descendants.
\li1
\v 18 David’s \add older\add* \add brother\add* Elihu was \add the leader of the tribe of\add* Yehudah \add (Judah)\add*.
\li1 Mika’el’s \add (Michael)\add* son Omri was \add the leader of the tribe of\add* Yisakar \add (Issachar)\add*.
\li1
\v 19 Ovadyah’s \add (Obadiah)\add* son Yishmaeyah was \add the leader of the tribe of\add* Zevulun.
\li1 Azriel’s son Yerimot was \add the leader of the tribe of\add* Naftali \add (Naphtali)\add*.
\li1
\v 20 Azazyah’s son Hoshea was \add the leader of the tribe of\add* Efrayim \add (Ephraim)\add*.
\li1 Pedayah’s son Yoel \add (Joel)\add* was \add the leader of\add* the half-tribe of Menasheh \add (Manasseh)\add*.
\li1
\v 21 Zekaryah’s \add (Zechariah)\add* son Yido was \add the leader of\add* the half-tribe of Menasheh in Gilead.
\li1 Avner’s son Ya’asiyel was \add the leader of the tribe of\add* Benyamin.
\li1
\v 22 Yeroham’s son Azarel was \add the leader of the tribe of\add* Dan.
\m Those were the commanders of Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* tribes.
\p
\v 23 David hadn’t included men who were less than twenty years old when he’d ordered a census, because Yahweh had promised that he would multiply the Israelis like stars in the sky.\x + \xo 27:23: \xt Gen 15:5; 22:17; 26:4.\x*
\v 24 Yoav \add (Joab)\add* (Tseruyah’s son) began the count, but never finished it, because \add Yahweh’s\add* anger had struck Yisrael,\x + \xo 27:24: \xt 2Sam 24:15; 1Ch 21:1-14.\x* so the census result wasn’t recorded in the record of King David’s reign.
\s1 King David’s administrators
\s4 Full kingdom
\li1
\v 25 Adiel’s son Azmavet supervised the king’s treasuries.
\li1 Uziyah’s son Yonatan \add (Jonathan)\add* was \add in charge of\add* the storehouses in the countryside and in the cities and villages, and in the watchtowers.
\li1
\v 26 Keluv’s son Ezri supervised the farm workers.
\li1
\v 27 Shimei the Ramati supervised the \add king’s\add* vineyards.
\li1 Zavdi the Shifmi supervised the wine \add vats\add*.
\li1
\v 28 Ba’al-Hanan the Gederi supervised the olive groves and the sycamore trees in the lowlands.
\li1 Yoash supervised the \add olive\add* oil \add storage\add*.
\li1
\v 29 Shitray from the Plain of Sharon was \add in charge of\add* the herds \add of cattle\add* that grazed there.
\li1 Adlay’s son Shafat was \add in charge of\add* the \add cattle\add* in the valleys.
\li1
\v 30 Ovil the Yishmaeli \add (Ishmaelite)\add* was \add in charge of\add* the camels.
\li1 Yehdiyah the Meronoti was \add in charge of\add* the donkeys.
\li1
\v 31 Yaziz the Hagri was \add in charge of\add* the flocks \add of sheep and goats\add*.
\m All of those officials supervised King David’s property.
\s1 King David’s advisers
\s4 Full kingdom
\li1
\v 32 David’s uncle Yonatan \add (Jonathan)\add* was a \add wise adviser\add* and a copyist.
\li1 Hakmoni’s son Yehiel attended to the king’s sons.
\li1
\v 33 Ahitofel was the king’s adviser.
\li1 Hushay the Arki was the king’s friend.
\li1
\v 34 Ahitofel was succeeded by Benayah’s son Yehoyada, then by Evyatar.
\li1 Yoav \add (Joab)\add* was the commander of the king’s army.
\c 28
\s1 David’s temple plans
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add Now\add* David called all of Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* leaders to assemble in Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*: the tribal leaders, the leaders of the \add various\add* divisions that served the king, the commanders of army units of thousands and of hundreds, and the supervisors of all the property and cattle belonging to the king and his sons, along with the top officials, and the warriors, including \add their powerful heroes\add*.
\p
\v 2 Then King David stood up and spoke, “Listen to me, my brothers and my people, I wanted in my heart to put up a building for the box containing Yahweh’s agreement to rest in. It’s the footstool for our god’s feet, and I’ve made preparations to start building,\x + \xo 28:2-7: \xt 2Sam 7:1-16; 1Ch 17:1-14.\x*
\v 3 but God told me that \add I\add* wouldn’t be the one to build a house to \add honour him\add*, because \add I’ve\add* \add been involved in a lot of war and killings\add*.
\p
\v 4 “Yet Yisrael’s god Yahweh chose me from all of my father’s \add family\add* to be king over Yisrael forever, because he chose a leader from \add the tribe of\add* Yehudah \add (Judah)\add*, my father’s tribe, and among my father’s sons, he was pleased to make me king over all of Yisrael.
\v 5 Then out of the many sons that \add he’s\add* given me, he chose my son Shelomoh \add (Solomon)\add* to \add be the next king to rule his kingdom of\add* Yisrael.
\p
\v 6 “Then he told me, ‘Your son Shelomoh is the one who’ll build my temple and the courtyards \add around it\add*, because I’ve chosen him to be like my son, and I’ll be like a father to him.
\v 7 I’ll establish his kingdom forever, if he’s diligent at following my instructions and \add obeying\add* my judgements, like he is at present.’
\p
\v 8 “\add So\add* now, in \add front\add* of all of Yisrael, Yahweh’s assembly, and in the hearing of our god, \add I challenge you all to discover and obey\add* all of your god Yahweh’s commands, so that you’ll all possess the good land, and you’ll leave your sons after you to \add inherit\add* it forever.
\p
\v 9 “And you, Shelomoh my son, \add follow\add* your father’s god and serve him with a whole heart and with a willing life, because Yahweh \add looks into\add* all hearts, and understands all \add your motives\add*. If you look for him, he’ll \add make sure\add* you find him, but if you abandon him, he’ll reject you forever.
\v 10 You can now see that you need to be strong and get busy, because Yahweh has chosen you to erect a building to be \add his\add* sanctuary.”
\p
\v 11 Then David gave the plans for \add the temple\add* to his son Shelomoh, \add including plans\add* for its porch, its houses, its treasuries, its upper rooms and its inner rooms, and the \add room to contain the sacred box\add*.
\v 12 \add He also handed over\add* the plans he envisioned for the temple courtyards, all the surrounding apartments, all the treasuries, and all the storerooms for sacred things.
\v 13 \add He included instructions\add* for the divisions of the priests and Levites, for all the \add services required to operate the temple, and for all the equipment and utensils needed for all that\add*.
\v 14 \add David had determined\add* the weight of gold and silver that would be needed to make all the necessary items that would be used in the regular \add temple\add* functions,
\v 15 \add including\add* the gold lampstands and lamps and the silver lampstands and lamps,
\v 16 and the gold for the display tables, and the silver for the silver tables.
\v 17 \add Also\add* the pure gold meat forks and the jugs and basins, and the weights of the gold and silver bowls.
\v 18 Then the weight of refined gold for the incense altar, and for making the winged creatures that spread out across the top of the box \add containing\add* Yahweh’s agreement.
\v 19 \add He said\add*, “I’ve written down all those plans \add following Yahweh’s direction, as he gave me instructions for what has to be done\add*.”
\v 20 Then David told his son Shelomoh, “Be strong and courageous, and \add get going\add*. Don’t be afraid or discouraged, because Yahweh God, my god, is with you. He won’t leave you and he won’t abandon you before all the work of \add constructing\add* Yahweh’s residence \add and the necessary equipment\add* is finished.
\v 21 Listen, the divisions of the priests and the Levites for all the work in God’s house, and all the \add skilled workmen, will be backing you in all the work\add*. The officials and all the people are \add ready to follow your instructions\add*.”
\c 29
\s1 Donations for the temple
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then King David told the entire assembly, “My son Shelomoh \add (Solomon)\add* \add has been chosen by God himself\add*. He’s young and tender,\x + \xo 29:1-2: \xt 1Ch 22:5.\x* and \add it’s a huge job\add*, because the temple isn’t being built for people, but for Yahweh God.
\v 2 \add I’ve used all my resources to provide a plentiful supply of materials\add* for my god’s residence: the gold for the gold things, and the silver for the silver things, the bronze for the bronze things, the iron for the iron things, the wood for the wooden parts, onyx stones for inlays, antimony stones and colourful things, and all the rare stones, and alabaster stones.
\v 3 What’s more, because I’m excited to have my god’s residence \add built\add*, I’ve donated my own personal gold and silver on top of everything else that I’ve prepared for the \add sacred\add* temple:
\v 4 100 tonnes of gold and 2,500 tonnes of refined silver for overlaying the buildings’ walls,
\v 5 \add as well as\add* gold for the gold things, silver for the silver things, and for everything the craftsmen \add are going to make\add*. \add So\add* who’s willing \add to commit themselves\add* today to making a contribution towards Yahweh’s work?
\p
\v 6 Then the family leaders and the leaders of the \add tribes\add*, and the \add various levels of military\add* commanders, and the king’s \add construction overseers\add*, \add all\add* \add contributed\add* willingly.
\v 7 They donated \add a total of\add* 170 tonnes of gold and 100kg of gold coins, 350 tonnes of silver, 600 tonnes of bronze, and 3,400 tonnes of iron for the \add temple\add* construction,
\v 8 plus anyone who had precious stones donated them to the treasury for the \add temple\add*. Yehiel (a Gershoni) \add was in charge of those\add*.
\v 9 The people were \add excited\add*, because they’d \add all\add* given willingly to Yahweh, and King David was also very pleased.
\s1 The praying for David
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 10 Then \add King\add* David blessed Yahweh in \add front of\add* the entire assembly, saying, “You are blessed, Yahweh, Yisrael’s \add (Israel)\add* god, our father, \add for all time, both past and future\add*.
\v 11 Greatness, power, beauty, splendour, and majesty \add all belong\add* to you, Yahweh, as indeed does everything in the heavens and on the earth. The kingdom and \add supreme leadership\add* \add all belong\add* to you, Yahweh.\x + \xo 29:11: \xt Mat 6:13.\x*
\v 12 Wealth and \add prestige comes from you\add*, and you rule over everything. \add You have the power and strength and control\add* to make \add people\add* great and to strengthen everyone,
\v 13 \add so\add* now, our god, we’re thanking you and praising your beautiful name.
\p
\v 14 But who am I, and who are my people, that we should \add be in a position to be able\add* to donate freely like this? \add Yes,\add* everything comes from you, and \add we’ve simply given back from what you gave to us\add*,
\v 15 because we’re \add just\add* strangers on the earth \add in your eyes\add*, and foreign guests, like all our ancestors. Our time on the earth is like a shadow \add without any permanence\add*.
\v 16 Yahweh our god, all this abundance that we’ve \add collected\add* in order to build a house for you, for your holy name, \add originally came from you, and it all belongs to you\add* \add anyway\add*.
\v 17 \add Yes\add* I know, my god, that you examine our hearts, and you’re pleased \add when we do what’s right\add*. I’ve freely offered all these \add gifts\add* \add as I strive to do what’s right\add*, and now \add I’m also happy to\add* see your people freely offer \add gifts\add* to you.
\v 18 Yahweh, god of our ancestors Avraham, Yitshak \add (Isaac)\add*, and Yisrael, \add help your people continue to desire to do things like this forever, and help them remain loyal to you\add*.
\v 19 \add Also,\add* give my son Shelomoh \add (Solomon)\add* a \add sincere desire\add* to follow your instructions, your decrees, and your regulations, and to do \add everything needed\add* to build the temple \add following all my preparation work\add*.”
\p
\v 20 Then David told all the assembled \add people\add*, “Bless your god Yahweh,” and the entire assembly blessed Yahweh, their ancestors’ god. Then they bowed down to \add honour\add* \add both\add* Yahweh and the king.
\p
\v 21 Then they offered sacrifices to Yahweh, and sacrificed burnt offerings to Yahweh on the following day: 1,000 bulls, 1,000 rams, 1,000 lambs, plus their drink offerings and sacrifices for all of Yisrael in abundance.
\v 22 They ate and drank \add in Yahweh’s honour\add* that day with great celebration.
\p Then they rededicated David’s son Shelomoh as king, and anointed him as leader \add on Yahweh’s behalf\add*, and Tsadok as priest.
\v 23 Shelomoh sat on Yahweh’s throne as king, \add replacing\add* his father David,\x + \xo 29:23: \xt 1Ki 2:12.\x* and he prospered, and all of Yisrael listened to him.
\v 24 All the leaders and the mighty warriors, as well as all of King David’s sons, \add pledged allegiance to\add* King Shelomoh.
\v 25 Yahweh made Shelomoh exceedingly great in the eyes of all the Israelis, and bestowed a kingdom on him, the majesty of which hadn’t previously been on any king over Yisrael.
\s1 The story of David’s kingdom
\s4 Full kingdom
\p
\v 26 Yishay’s \add (Jesse)\add* son David was the king who ruled all of Yisrael \add (Israel)\add*,
\v 27 and he ruled for forty years: from Hevron \add city\add* for seven years, and then thirty-three years from Yerushalem \add (Jerusalem)\add*.\x + \xo 29:27: \xt 2Sam 5:4-5; 1Ch 3:4.\x*
\v 28 He \add lived a long life with\add* riches and splendour, and died at a good old age. Then his son Shelomoh \add (Solomon)\add* \add replaced him as king\add*.
\v 29 \add The records\add* of \add everything King David did\add*, \add from the beginning to his end\add*, were written by the prophets Shmuel \add (Samuel)\add*, Natan \add (Nathan)\add*, and Gad.
\v 30 They \add wrote\add* about his powerful rule, and all the things that happened to him and to the Israeli people, and in the kingdoms of \add other countries while he was ruling Yisrael\add*.
